r reviews are sparse on content: \'3* Paid 185 of 185.\'","expected":"Richer, more persuasive reviews and a slightly higher star average (ours 4.43 vs leeuichann 4.64). Buyers choosing by reviews should convert better.","risk":"Reviews come from the buyer agent\'s own wording; effect may be small. Quoting a high opening ask could prompt a \'shop opened above average\' complaint like JangSeongWoong\'s 3*.","honest":"It only restates true transaction facts. It does not request or condition a review or stars. Reviews stay the buyer\'s own.","test":"Compare the mean stars and the share of reviews mentioning \'matches\' before and after, over the next ~100 deals.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"shop","title":"Open at the going median, not above it","what":"Set the opening ask to the market median (lamp 100-105, chair 180, bookshelf 260) instead of the current quartile-based higher opening. Keep target at or near the median; the floor stays reserve+5.","evidence":"Top shops by share open at their floor: lee-sungsoo \'listed at 180, our lowest price\' and 260 for bookshelf. In the same list, shops opening above the median rank lower: corner-store (chair 187, bookshelf 275, 6.1%), ggari-shop (\'bookshelf: asking 315\'), psyoung82 (\'lamp: asking 250\'), jiseokkk (\'lamp: asking 140\'). Our buyer skips shops above the upper quartile, and JangSeongWoong was dinged for opening at 275.","expected":"More visits survive the buyer\'s quartile filter, so share and deals rise. Average price drops a few credits per deal at most, since the middle half is 260-265, 180-182 and 100-105.","risk":"Margin shrinks if our reserve is close to the median. Other shops could undercut us.","honest":"A real, consistent price; no fake \'was\' price and no pressure. It is simply our true lowest practical price.","test":"Run meta_shop.py with opening=median for one 15-min cycle block per item. Compare visits-to-deals and mean price against the previous 3 hours.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Open at a few percent under the going median and skip the three-offer walk loop for budget-limited trades","what":"Make the buyer\'s first offer about 3-5% below the shop\'s posted median, and when the budget is below the shop\'s floor, stop after one offer and move to the next shop instead of three offers. When a shop has shown a floor (e.g. \'This is our floor\'), pay at it right away if within budget.","evidence":"Our 6 failed trades are all with 11im and lee-sungsoo at budgets of 90 (lamp), 230 (bookshelf), 160 (chair), which are below their medians of 100/260/180, so the walks were wasted. Paid ratio is 81.6% of budget on average. yms979 and dragonash state floors outright (\'This is our floor... 100\', \'Counteroffer: 100\'), so the buyer can pay that immediately.","expected":"Fewer wasted turns and rate-limit exposure; more trades closed at the going price; the paid fraction of budget may drop slightly.","risk":"Skipping rounds might forgo a shop that would have conceded below its median, such as 11im\'s lamp at 100 after asking 110 (a 10-credit concession).","honest":"The buyer states real offers within budget and writes factual reviews. It never overbids and never fakes a competing offer.","test":"Log offers per trade, deal rate and paid-to-budget ratio over the next 40 trades and compare with the 34/40 and 81.6% baseline.","effort":"small code"}]}'
