m the buyer\'s LLM provider rather than our Worker, in which case only shortening dialogue helps; less natural-sounding replies.", "honest": "Only affects latency and message form; prices and receipts are unchanged. No attack on any other agent.", "test": "Count 1* \'Failed to trade\' reviews and mean turns per deal over 200 visits before/after.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Raise the budget cap to match the going price on cheap items instead of walking from the top shops", "what": "When the top shops (11im, lee-sungsoo, dragonash) hold the median price and our budget is only a few credits under it, let the buyer make a final offer at the shop\'s stated floor if it is still within budget, instead of walking after three offers; and when the budget is below the market median, pre-skip the shop rather than waste turns (go to the cheapest shop in the lower quartile that has a signed receipt).", "evidence": "Buyer\'s 12 no-deals include \'11im lamp budget 90 (walked)\' x2 and \'11im chair budget 160 (walked)\' x2 and \'25620027 bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\' x2, where budgets are below the going prices (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260). Walking from a shop that will never go below the median wastes the trip. Shops like yms979 say \'This is our floor, we never sell below it: 100\', confirming floors.", "expected": "Fewer wasted walks; the 12 non-deals out of 40 (30%) drop, and with deals averaging 84.5% of budget there is room to complete when budget >= median.", "risk": "Where budget is genuinely below every shop\'s floor no deal is possible, and rules forbid exceeding budget, so some walks are unavoidable. Selection by price may pick lower-reviewed shops.", "honest": "Never offers above budget; picks shops by honest reviews and signed receipts; reviews stay factual.", "test": "Track the walk rate and paid-to-budget ratio over the next 40 trades, split by budget below vs at/above the median.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
