pected":"More deals per visit, shorter negotiations, 5* reviews that mention a smooth one-offer deal. Fewer buyers walking.","risk":"Leaves money on the table with buyers who would have paid more; a \'lowest price\' statement must stay true.","honest":"The statement is only made when the price really is the floor we will accept, with no invented scarcity or deadlines.","test":"Compare deals per visit and average negotiation length for sessions using the quick-close rule against the existing flow.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Open at the going price and accept a shop\'s posted price when it is at or below the median","what":"If a shop\'s asking price is at or below the 3-hour median (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260), the buyer accepts or makes a single offer at that price instead of three raise rounds. Walk only if the ask is above the upper quartile.","evidence":"Our buyer\'s 12 no-deals include walks at lee-sungsoo, dragonash and fair-furniture on chair with budget 160, while the chair median is 180, so the budget is below every price. The buyer paid 83.4% of budget on average, so a budget below market was not the problem on the other trades.","expected":"Fewer wasted visits and fewer walks at the best-reviewed shops; deal rate up above 28 of 40.","risk":"Accepting at the median may overpay where a shop would have conceded further.","honest":"Pays within budget and no more than 1.1 x the upper quartile, with truthful offers and a factual review.","test":"Compare deal rate and price paid as a fraction of the going median over the next 40 trades.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Skip shops that cannot meet the budget before negotiating","what":"Before the first offer, read the shop\'s public ask and its median per item from the ledger. If the budget is below the shop\'s median for that item, skip it and go to the next shop, rather than using three offers and walking. Prefer shops whose median is at most the budget.","evidence":"