 not backed by what the shop actually offers, it becomes a false claim; the line is only usable with real terms.","honest":"Only states facts the shop can honor, and the signed receipt is already what we issue.","test":"Compare conversion (deals/visits) for the 3 hours before and after the template change, same items.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Open with a spread-aware first offer near the going median","what":"For each item, make the first offer at about 95-97% of the going median (bookshelf ~250, chair ~172, lamp ~96) instead of budget-proportional, then raise up to 40% of the gap, and never go over budget or 1.1 x upper quartile. Walk only after three offers.","evidence":"Going prices have very tight middle-half bands (chair 180-182, lamp 100-103) and top shops like dragonash and lee-sungsoo close at 180/260/100. Our buyer paid only 84.1% of budget on average but 10 of the last 40 trades ended without a deal, several \'walked\' at budget 160 on chair against a 180 median.","expected":"Fewer pointless walks, with price paid near the market median; deal rate up from 75%.","risk":"A low budget (160 for a chair) cannot reach 180, so those trades stay no-deals; a median-based opener could overpay buyers with budget slack.","honest":"Offers are our own real bids within budget, no false claims about other offers.","test":"Run 40 trades with the new opener vs the last 40; compare deal rate and price/median.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Skip shops with a no-deal history for the same budget band","what":"Add to buyer memory a per-shop, per-item record of walk-aways at a given budget. When the budget is below the shop\'s lowest recent deal price for that item, route to a shop that has closed at or under that budget (e.g. ggari-shop, psyoung82, corner-store lamp at 105) instead of trying shops that held the floor.","evidence":"Our buyer walked at lee-sungsoo chair budget 160 twice, dragonash chair 160, fair-furniture bookshelf 230 tw