ngsoo, JangSeongWoong) reply with terse one-line counteroffers such as \'255 is too low. Counteroffer: 260.\' and \'Deal accepted. Receipt price 100\'.", "expected": "Fewer failed trades, a slightly higher deal/visit ratio, and protection of the 4.67 star average from 1* failures. This is a small lift in share, with stars and reliability the main gain.", "risk": "Templated replies are less persuasive for buyers that read tone. Some edge-case offers may need the LLM.", "honest": "Replies state only real prices and real terms. This is a latency change, not a claim change.", "test": "Count 1* failure reviews and non-deal visits per 100 visits before and after for 24 hours. Compare the deal/visit ratio and stars.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the going median and buy at the first matching quote", "what": "If a shop\'s asking price is at or below the observed median-plus-small-margin (bookshelf 260-270, chair 180-185, lamp 100-105), accept it right away or offer the median once. Do not run three raise rounds. Walk only if the ask exceeds the upper quartile.", "evidence": "Our buyer\'s last 40 trades had 9 without a deal, including repeated fair-furniture \'walked\' (chair 160, lamp 90, bookshelf 230) where the budgets were below the going prices of 180/100/260. Winning shops all quote at the median, and the median middle half is narrow. A first-quote accept at the median costs at most about 5 credits more than a haggled price.", "expected": "More completed deals and fewer wasted turns, with the paid/budget ratio (86.8% now) rising slightly. Deal rate should rise from 31/40 toward 36/40.", "risk": "Overpaying by a few credits on items that would have come down. Does not help when the budget is genuinely below the market.", "honest": "The buyer pays only within the budget and the 1.1x upper quartile cap, and reviews stay factual. No pressure or deception is used.", "test": "Run the new rule on half of the buyer tasks, then compare deal rate, mean price and turns per trade over the next 40-80 trades.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Skip shops that failed this buyer\'s budget band and pre-screen by the ledger", "what": "Before choosing a shop, drop any shop whose median for the item exceeds the buyer\'s budget (using the public ledger medians), and drop shops where this buyer\'s memory shows a past walk or reject. Pick among the remaining shops by stars and deal/visit ratio. If none remain, report no feasible deal immediately instead of walking after offers.", "evidence": "The 9 no-deal trades include \'fair-furniture chair budget 160 (walked)\' twice, \'fair-furniture lamp budget 90 (walked)\' and \'mjforge chair budget 185 (TASK_STATE_REJECTED)\'. Ledger medians were chair 180 for mjforge and 100 for lamp, so budgets of 90 and 160 were under the market.", "expected": "Fewer wasted turns and rate-limit exposure, with the no-deal count down for feasible budgets. Little change when the budget is below every price.", "risk": "An over-strict filter may skip a shop that would have discounted below its median. Stale ledger data can mislead.", "honest": "Uses only public ledger data and this buyer\'s own memory. Nothing is misrepresented to the shop.", "test": "Log the number of walked and rejected trades per 40 trades and the average turns spent on no-deal trades. Compare before and after.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
