ith 100 alone for instant signed receipt\') and 25620027 (\'Reply with exactly 262 to complete the purchase\') use this and hold ~4% share with 4.41 stars; our reviews score \'speed\' (e.g. \'2 offers\' got 5/5 speed vs \'3 offers\' 4/5).", "expected": "Fewer negotiation rounds, higher speed sub-score in auto-reviews, and fewer buyers walking from slow haggling; modest star and deal-rate gain.", "risk": "Buyers who would have paid more at the ask accept earlier than needed only if ask is lower than their budget (already true today), so little loss; risk is buyer agents that ignore the instruction.", "honest": "The quoted price is exactly what we will sign on the receipt; nothing is hidden or conditional.", "test": "Compare average offers per deal, speed score in reviews, and deals/visits before and after on the same items for 24h.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Blacklist/deprioritize shops that caused our walks and prefer proven median-price shops", "what": "Add to the buyer\'s memory a per-shop-and-item \'walk count\' and \'paid vs budget\' record; after two walks at a shop for an item at the same budget, rank that shop last for that item for a few hours. First choice goes to shops whose recent medians for the item are at or below the market median (11im, dragonash, lee-sungsoo, mjforge, ggari-shop for chair/bookshelf).", "evidence": "Last 40 trades: 10 without a deal, repeated walks at fair-furniture (bookshelf 230, chair 160 twice) and mjforge lamp 90 twice; fair-furniture budgets (160 chair, 230 bookshelf) are well below the going medians 180 and 260. Top shops\' medians sit at 100/180/260.", "expected": "Fewer wasted trades, deal rate above 30/40, and no loss in price paid; saves turns for shops that actually clear.", "risk": "Memory could penalize a shop that only failed because of an unusually low budget, and shift too much volume to the top three shops.", "honest": "It only uses our own observed outcomes and public ledger data; no reviews are written or conditioned, and no one is attacked.", "test": "Track deal rate and mean paid/budget over the next 40 trades versus the last 40, split by whether the shop was chosen by the new rule.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Budget-aware first offer at the market median", "what": "When our budget is at or above the going median, open at the median (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) instead of a lower anchor, and accept a counter at or below the upper quartile. When the budget is below the median (lamp 90, chair 160, bookshelf 230), make one polite offer at the budget to the shop with the lowest median and walk quickly rather than spend three offers.", "evidence": "Top shops\' counters cluster at the median: dragonash \'90 is too low. Counteroffer: 100\', \'180 is too low. Counteroffer 181\', JangSeongWoong \'Last offer 259\', mjforge \'No deal at 256/179\'. Our buyer pays 84.8% of budget on average, yet our walks occur at budgets far under the median, where three offers cannot succeed.", "expected": "Higher deal rate for budgets at or above median (fewer rounds), and less time spent on hopeless budgets; price paid stays near median, within 1.1x upper quartile.", "risk": "Opening at the median forgoes a few credits in cases where the shop would have accepted a lower first offer.", "honest": "Offers never exceed our budget and are real; we make no false claims to shops and do not touch reviews except to report facts.", "test": "Compare deals per offer round, mean paid/budget, and walk rate over 40 trades for the new policy versus the previous 40.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
