[15:35:54] HARNESS: try 1: 21.8 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas":[{"side":"shop","title":"Exact-price one-reply close at the ask","what":"When our current ask is at or within a few credits of the buyer\'s last offer, state the ask plainly and tell the buyer that replying with exactly that number completes the purchase and returns a signed receipt. Add this line to the Worker reply template and the code-side counteroffer path. Keep the concession rule unchanged; this only shortens the final step.","evidence":"mjforge: \'listed asking price: 180 (reply with 180 alone for instant signed receipt)\'; 25620027: \'Reply with exactly 262 to complete the purchase\'; and our own reviews, where buyers who ran 4 to 6 offers at budget (e.g. \'completed at 105; budget was 105... Actual offers: 6\') gave only 3 stars.","expected":"Fewer offers per deal, which raises the speed score in buyer reviews and moves some 3-star reviews to 4-5. Deals may rise a little as fewer buyers walk after round three. Price is unchanged.","risk":"Buyers who would have haggled lower pay the ask, so average price rises by a small amount. The Llama worker might also mis-handle a bare number reply.","honest":"It states only our real ask and what the exact reply does. No scarcity, deadline or fake \'was\' price is involved, and the price never falls below reserve + 5.","test":"A/B by deal id parity over 24 hours. Compare average offers per deal, review stars, deals per visit and the average price between the two arms.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"shop","title":"Explain the price in the review-facing receipt message","what":"Put one factual line in the deal message and the signed receipt text: the item, the agreed price, the number of offers, and where the price sits against the current market median that meta_shop.py already computes. Example: \'Agreed 260; market median 260 (last 3h); 2 offers; receipt signed.\' The line must come from live data and be omitted if the data is stale.","evidence":"Buyer reviews quote the numbers they find, such as mjforge\'s \'[deal] bookshelf at 260 | price 5/5 (median 275) | speed 4/5 (3 offers) | receipt 5/5\' and JangSeongWoong\'s \'Opened at 115 (about 10% above the 104.2 market average)\'. Buyer agents score price against the median and the offer count, so giving them verified figures makes a 5-star score easier to reach.","expected":"Higher average stars, from 4.71 toward the 4.8 range. Better stars improve our rank in buyer selection, so share should rise slowly from 2.7%.","risk":"If our price sits above the median at some moment, the line highlights that. The extra text also adds a little token cost.","honest":"Every figure is true and taken from the market data. It does not ask for any particular star rating and does not pay for or condition reviews; the existing honest-review request stays as it is.","test":"Compare mean stars and the share of 5-star reviews for deals before and after the change, matched on item and hour. Also check review text for citations of the figures.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Open near the going price and skip the three-offer walk when the budget is within 5% of the median","what":"Change the buyer so that when the budget is at or above the quartile-median price for the item, it first offers a value close to the median (about 98 to 100%) and accepts the first counteroffer at or below the median + 2. Keep the 40% raise cap, the 1.1 x upper quartile ceiling and the shop filters. When the budget is below every shop\'s price, make one offer and walk instead of three.","evidence":"Going prices are tight: lamp 100-102, chair 180-181, bookshelf 260-265, and dragonash and ggari-shop answer low offers with their floor (\'156 is too low. Counteroffer: 180\', \'same for every buyer\'). Our buyer logged 12 non-deals out of 40, several of them \'walked\' at budgets below the market (lamp budget 90, chair budget 160, bookshelf 230) while paying 84.3% of budget on average.","expected":"The deal rate rises on feasible budgets, because low-ball rounds are wasted against fixed-floor shops. Fewer wasted calls also help against the 429 rate-limit failures seen in our reviews. The average price paid is about the same.","risk":"Against shops that really do concede (fair-furniture, 11im), opening high costs a few credits per deal.","honest":"The buyer makes real offers within its own budget and states facts in its reviews. It does not mislead any shop about its budget.","test":"Run old and new offer policies on alternating orders for 40 trades each. Compare the deal rate, the price as a share of the median, and the number of offers per trade.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"shop","title":"Bounded rate-limit retry and short replies to cut harness failures","what":"Shorten the Worker\'s system prompt and reply length, cache the pricing context, and handle simple offers in code (accept, counter or floor) without calling the model. Reserve the LLM for free-text questions. Add a short, jittered retry with a clear fallback message if a model call fails.","evidence":"Two of the eight reviews we have are 1-star: \'Failed to trade: RuntimeError: Harness failed after 5 attempts for buyer_turn: Error code: 429 - Rate limit reached\'. A third says \'No validated purchase completed for this order. No deal recorded.\' Each such failure lowers stars and loses a visit; we convert 1972 of 2699 visits (73%) against dragonash\'s 79%.","expected":"A higher visit-to-deal conversion rate, closer to 78%, which means roughly 5% more deals at the same traffic. It also removes the 1-star outliers that pull stars down.","risk":"Code-only handling may miss unusual buyer phrasing. Retries must stay small so we do not load the harness.","honest":"It changes only our own reliability. It makes no claims, does not touch reviews, and does not call or load other agents.","test":"Track the failure and timeout rate per 100 visits and the visit-to-deal ratio for 24 hours before and after. Count 1-star reviews that mention a 429 error.","effort":"small code"}]}'
