pleted at 265; budget was 270... Actual offers: 2\'). Rival 5* reviews quote concrete facts: fair-furniture \'ask fell from 273 to 265 to 260, I paid 260... signed by the shop\'; ggari-shop\'s review scores price, speed and receipt, citing the median. Our stars are 4.53 against fair-furniture 4.61 and 11im 4.58.", "expected": "Buyers score price and receipt from the facts we state, which lifts the stars by about 0.05-0.1. Higher stars should lift selection by review-reading buyers over time.", "risk": "Buyer reviewers may ignore the text. Quoting the median could expose a price above it, which would draw a lower score.", "honest": "Only true, verifiable facts go in. The message does not ask for stars or condition anything on a review.", "test": "Compare mean stars and the share of 3-star reviews before and after over about 500 deals.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Hold bookshelf and chair at the cluster price and drop the opening ask", "what": "Set the opening ask for each item to the middle-half low end (bookshelf 260, chair 180, lamp 100) and accept at or above it. Concede only if the code can stay at or above reserve+5. This replaces the current quartile-derived ask, which may sit near the top of the band.", "evidence": "Going prices: middle half bookshelf 260-265, chair 180-181, lamp 100-105. The top shops (lee-sungsoo, dragonash, mjforge, JangSeongWoong) all have medians of exactly 260/180/100. Shops that ask above the band (yms979 at 188 chair, 272 bookshelf, 4.23 stars, 4.1%; 11im chair 195) have lower share, and our own deals sit at 265-268.", "expected": "Share rises toward the 11-13% of the leaders, since buyers skip shops above the upper quartile. Margin per deal falls by about 3-8 credits.", "risk": "Margin shrinks if reserve is close to the band. Volume may not respond if buyers rank on reviews rather than price.", "honest": "Real lower prices, no false \'was\' price, and the reserve floor is kept.", "test": "Run for 2 hours against the control ask. Compare share, deals/visits and total margin (deals x (price - reserve)).", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Make a first offer of the going-price floor and accept a close counter at once", "what": "When a shop has historically sold near the median, open at the middle-half low end (bookshelf 260, chair 180, lamp 100). If the counter is within the budget and at or below the upper quartile, accept immediately rather than make the three-offer walk. Walk only when the counter exceeds both the budget and the cap.", "evidence": "9 of the last 40 trades ended without a deal, and the walks are against the very shops that sell at the median: fair-furniture (budget 160 for a chair, 90 for a lamp, 230 for a bookshelf), lee-sungsoo bookshelf 230, dragonash chair 160. The going prices are 180 chair, 100 lamp and 260 bookshelf, so those budgets were below every shop\'s price. The deals paid 85.1% of budget, which leaves room.", "expected": "Fewer wasted visits and a higher deal rate on orders whose budget reaches the median. For orders with a budget below the median, a walk right away saves the three-offer exchange. Price paid stays close to the median.", "risk": "Skipping the haggle can mean paying 2-5 above what a patient counter would reach. Early acceptance on cheap budgets could cost a few credits.", "honest": "The buyer never goes above budget, and its reviews stay factual. It does not misstate its budget or invent other offers.", "test": "Compare deal rate and the price paid relative to the median over 40-60 trades against the current policy.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
