[20:38:00] HARNESS: try 1: 18.0 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas": [{"side": "shop", "title": "Quote a firm price on first reply, then match the buyer\'s own offer immediately", "what": "Open at the going median (bookshelf 260-262, chair 180, lamp 100-101) and tell the buyer the price is final and that replying with the exact price returns a signed receipt at once. Accept any offer at or above the target in a single turn, with no multi-round concession. Keep reserve + 5 as the floor. This is a prompt/template change in the Worker plus a lower opening-ask knob in meta_shop.py.", "evidence": "Our reviews are mostly 3* and mention \'Actual offers: 3\' or 2 on bookshelves at 265-268 while top shops close in 1-3 offers at 260 (ggari-shop \'reply ok and the signed receipt comes back at once\', mjforge \'reply with 180 alone for instant signed receipt\', fair-furniture 5* \'after 1 offer(s)\'). Our stars are 4.51 against fair-furniture\'s 4.61, and our share is 2.6% on 3013 visits against dragonash\'s 13% on 14077.", "expected": "Higher visit-to-deal conversion (ours 74% vs dragonash 79%), fewer 3* speed and price comments, stars up toward 4.6. A slightly lower average price per deal is likely, offset by volume.", "risk": "We leave a few credits on the table on buyers with high budgets. If the opening is set below target, margin can fall near reserve + 5.", "honest": "The quoted price is the true price we charge. No fake scarcity, no invented \'was\' price, and no sale below reserve.", "test": "A/B by item for 24h: firm-quote template vs current. Compare deals/visits, average review stars and mean offers per deal.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Fix the \'no validated purchase\' failure by confirming the receipt in the closing message", "what": "In the closing message, repeat item, quantity, agreed price and the signed receipt id in one line, and make the Worker re-send the signed receipt if the buyer replies again. Log each deal where the buyer does not send a confirming turn.", "evidence": "Two reviews of our shop read 3* \'No validated purchase completed for this order. No deal recorded.\' Round 2 counts only signed receipts. dragonash\'s review also says \'receipt matches the agreement\' and \'market signature validation pending\', which shows buyers grade the receipt.", "expected": "Fewer 3* no-deal reviews and fewer lost Round 2 deals. A small stars gain (about 0.02-0.05) and a small share gain.", "risk": "Extra text in replies adds tokens and a little latency on the 8B model.", "honest": "This only reports what actually happened and makes the signed receipt easier to verify.", "test": "Count reviews containing \'No validated purchase\' per 100 deals before and after, and compare receipts_with_order against deals.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Stop retrying shops that walked us repeatedly; route to the cheapest fast-closing shop", "what": "Add a per-shop, per-item cool-down: after 2 walks with the same shop and item, skip that shop for 3 hours. Choose among shops within the lower quartile by deal rate, using public deal/visit ratios and first-price clarity, rather than by stars alone. Raise the budget cap on bookshelves only up to the shop\'s firm quote, which stays inside 1.1 x upper quartile.", "evidence": "Of 9 trades with no deal, most were fair-furniture walks at budgets of 160 (chair), 230 (bookshelf, three times) and 90 (lamp), below the going prices of 180, 260 and 100. Budgets below the market median cannot succeed, yet the buyer kept returning. Other shops close at the median: ggari-shop \'Our final price: 260\' and psyoung82 \'Purchased chair at 180\'.", "expected": "Fewer wasted walks (9 of 40 now), higher deal rate above 78%. Average paid share of budget would rise from 83%, which is acceptable because completed trades matter more.", "risk": "Skipping shops lowers the diversity of our own reviews. A shop might be at a price spike when we return.", "honest": "The buyer only avoids shops that did not close with it and still pays only prices a shop actually quotes, never exceeding the budget.", "test": "Compare deal rate over the next 40 trades against the last 40 (31/40) and track the count of repeat walks.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Cut the opening ask on bookshelves from about 265-268 to 262 with a 1-2 credit reduction", "what": "Set meta_shop.py so bookshelf opening is the market\'s middle-half upper bound (262) and the target is 260. Keep chair at 180-181 and lamp at 100-101. Disable the multi-step concession for bookshelves so there is a single clear price.", "evidence": "Our reviews show bookshelf deals at 265 and 268 with 2-3 offers, while the market bookshelf median is 260 (middle half 260-262), and 11im closes at 262 and dragonash at 260. Buyers pick shops by price and reviews, and the buyer skips shops above the upper quartile.", "expected": "Fewer 3* price complaints and higher bookshelf conversion. Average bookshelf revenue falls by about 3-5 credits per deal and is likely offset by volume.", "risk": "A race to the median reduces margin, and if the reserve is close to 260 the margin on bookshelves becomes thin.", "honest": "We charge a real, lower, current price. No false anchor and no sale below reserve + 5.", "test": "Run for 6 hours on bookshelves only and compare bookshelf deals/visits and stars against the previous 6 hours and against chairs and lamps as controls.", "effort": "knob"}]}'
