[21:38:24] HARNESS: try 1: 17.9 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas": [{"side": "shop", "title": "Honest-quality fact line in the deal message", "what": "Append one true, specific line to each signed-receipt message, e.g. the item\'s real material or dimensions from our catalog, in the style of fair-furniture (\'solid beech wood\'). Keep it short, no invented provenance or story. Keep the existing honest-review request.", "evidence": "fair-furniture has the highest stars (4.61) with 4816 deals, and its messages cite \'solid beech wood\'. Reviews of our shop are mostly 3*, and they only restate the price and budget (\'Purchase completed at 105; budget was 105\'), so buyers have no product detail to praise.", "expected": "Stars up from 4.5 toward 4.6 and a small share gain as reviews improve. No price change.", "risk": "Buyer agents may ignore it. The extra text adds a little latency or tokens. A fact that doesn\'t match the real item would be a false claim.", "honest": "States only true attributes of items we actually sell. It doesn\'t ask for or condition any review, and it makes no claim about scarcity, other buyers or a \'was\' price.", "test": "A/B by order parity for 24h. Compare average stars and the share of 3* reviews between the two arms, and check that deal rate is unchanged.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Hold the ask at the market median instead of opening above it", "what": "In meta_shop.py, cap the opening ask at the going median (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) and set target equal to the median. Currently our receipts show lamps at 105 and bookshelves at 265, above the middle-half band. Keep the reserve+5 floor.", "evidence": "Going prices are lamp 100 (middle half 100-103), chair 180 and bookshelf 260. Our reviews show \'completed at 105\' and \'completed at 265\', and several buyers had budgets of exactly 105 or 110. The top three shops all post median 100/180/260, and the buyer skips shops above the upper quartile. Our share is 2.6% on 3031 visits versus 13.1% for dragonash.", "expected": "More visits converted and more buyer picks, since we\'d no longer be filtered out as above-quartile. Share up, with a few percent less revenue per deal.", "risk": "Margin shrinks if the reserve is close to the median. Share may not move if reviews are the bottleneck.", "honest": "Just a lower honest price, never below reserve+5. No false \'was\' price is shown.", "test": "Run for 3 hours after a change and compare visits-to-deals and deals per hour with the previous 3 hours. Track average margin over reserve.", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the market median and raise in smaller steps to avoid walking", "what": "Change the buyer\'s rule from \'three offers then walk when budget is below every price\' to \'if the budget is at or above the going median, make up to five offers and finish at the median.\' Cap each raise at 25% of the gap rather than 40%. If the budget is below the median, try the shops with the lowest observed deal prices first, and walk only after those.", "evidence": "Of the 7 no-deals, the lamp walks are budget 90 at dragonash and fair-furniture, and 90 at 11im (None). Lamp median is 100, so those budgets were below every price. A 5* review at dragonash notes a chair deal after 5 offers at 180 (72% of budget). Our buyer\'s 85.3% of budget paid is high against that.", "expected": "Lower average price paid (target under 80% of budget) and fewer walks on budgets that are reachable. Walks where budget < median stay unchanged.", "risk": "More offers per deal take longer and raise load on shops. Shops may ignore small increments.", "honest": "No claims are made to the shop, and offers never exceed budget. It\'s ordinary negotiation with a limited number of messages.", "test": "Compare over the next 40 trades: deal rate, paid as a fraction of budget, and offers per deal against the current 33/40 and 85.3%.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Honest loyalty line with a receipt code, like 11im", "what": "Replace our generic loyalty text with a single line giving the code and the exact benefit we really honor, e.g. \'Receipt No. XXXX: mention it next visit for 3% off\', and make sure the discount is honored in code. Keep the existing tiers and stamp card for deeper loyalty.", "evidence": "11im has stars 4.58, 10014 deals and 11.5% share, and puts \'Receipt No. YXPVFPEW: mention it on your next visit for an extra 3% off\' in every deal message. It converts 10014 of 15295 visits despite a chair median of 195. lee-sungsoo\'s stamp card (\'Send 4 different unused LSS stamps\') is another repeat-buyer hook. Our 2244 deals are mostly one-time visits.", "expected": "Higher repeat visits and a modest share gain. The cost is a 3% discount on repeat orders only.", "risk": "Repeat discounts could push a price below reserve+5 if stacked with tiers. Buyers may not read the line.", "honest": "The discount is real, honored every time, and never stacked below reserve. There\'s no urgency or scarcity claim and no review condition.", "test": "Count orders carrying a code from a prior deal, compare repeat-visit rate before and after over 24h, and check margin on those orders.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
