gher quartile. Keep the reserve + 5 limit. Lamp stays at 100-102 as it is.", "evidence": "Market medians are chair 180 and bookshelf 260. Our reviews say \'chair at 187 | price 3/5 (median 180)\', \'Bought chair at 185 (budget 225)\' and \'chair: came down 192->185, then held ... its floor was above my limit\'. A JangSeongWoong review criticizes an opening of 195 (average 186.1). The top shops with a 4.5+ star rating sell at the median.", "expected": "Price ratings should rise from 3/5 to 4/5 or 5/5 on chairs, and stars about +0.1 for chairs. Chair and bookshelf deal conversion should rise, which lifts share. The margin cost is 3-5 credits per item.", "risk": "A thinner margin per item. If our reserve is close to 180 the room for the ask may be too small. Volume might not make up for it.", "honest": "Only prices change. Nothing is claimed beyond the actual ask and we stay above reserve + 5.", "test": "Set the knob for chair and bookshelf only for 48 hours. Compare the price sub-score in reviews, deals per visit and gross margin against the previous 48 hours, per item.", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Short, verifiable reply with the receipt in one message", "what": "Make the Llama reply a fixed template when the deal closes: \'Deal: <item> at <price>. Closed in <n> offers. Your receipt is signed by the shop, so this deal counts. Please leave an honest review.\' The code fills in the numbers from the actual negotiation state. Keep the loyalty code in a second line. Any non-template wording goes through the code, not the 8B model.", "evidence": "dragonash (12.6% share, 12056 reviews for 12056 deals) closes with exactly this kind of line, and its reviews say \'receipt matches the agreement\'. Our 4* reviews describe the receipt but only one mentions the \'Ed25519 receipt verified\'. Round 2 counts only signed receipts, so stating clearly that the receipt is signed helps buyer agents that check for it.", "expected": "Fewer mismatches between text and receipt, steady \'receipt 5/5\' scores, and a slightly higher review rate per deal (we have 1.0, so mostly stable). Stars should move from 4.51 toward 4.6 on fewer 4* reviews with price remarks.", "risk": "A rigid template can read as cold and lose the \'polite\' remarks that some buyers give. Little effect if buyers already verify the signature themselves.", "honest": "The sentences are factual (the receipt is signed, the offer count is true) and the review request is the same neutral honest-review ask as now.", "test": "Switch half of closing messages to the template. Compare receipt sub-scores, star averages and review count per deal between the arms over 500 deals each.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Stop retrying shops that walked us, and drop shops that only sell above budget", "what": "In the buyer\'s memory, record \'walked\' outcomes per shop and item with the budget. Before choosing a shop, skip any shop that has already walked on the same item at or above the current budget in the last few hours. Prefer shops whose public median for that item is within the budget (the market lamp floor is 100, so a budget of 90 can only win with a shop that has posted something lower). If every price is above the budget, return at once instead of making three offers.", "evidence": "Of the last 10 no-deals, ggari-shop lamp budget 90 walked twice, cheapest-in-town lamp budget 105 walked, 25620027 lamp budget 90 walked and fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230 walked. The going lamp price is 100 (middle half 100-102), so budget 90 cannot clear it. ggari-shop says outright \'no shop here may sign below it\', so retrying it is pointless.", "expected": "Fewer wasted trades (10 of 40 ended without a deal), less time spent per task, no change in average price paid (83.4% of budget) on deals that happen. If we sometimes raise the budget ceiling within the allowed 1.1 x upper quartile, a few of those lamp trades could close.", "risk": "A shop may change its price 