 ledger data. The reviews it writes stay factual. No budget is exceeded.", "test": "Log walks per 40 trades before and after. Compare deal rate, percent of budget paid and number of offers per visit.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Prefer shops that quote the best signed-receipt price: direct close at the shop\'s own price", "what": "When a shop\'s first ask is at or under the median and within budget, the buyer accepts it at once rather than haggling three rounds. When the ask is above, it makes one counter at the median and then follows the existing 40%-gap rule. This saves offers and cuts the rejections seen at ggari-shop.", "evidence": "ggari-shop chair budget 185 ended in TASK_STATE_REJECTED, though ggari-shop\'s ask for a chair is 180. Top shops sell at the median anyway: ggari-shop \'asked 180; I paid 180\', cheapest-in-town \'opened at 180, sold at 180\', 25620027 \'Fixed minimum: 260; no lower bids\'.", "expected": "Fewer failed negotiations at fixed-price shops and fewer rejections. Faster deals at about the same price paid.", "risk": "Some shops would have accepted less than the median, so a few credits might be left on the table.", "honest": "The buyer pays at most its budget and the ceiling of 1.1 x the upper quartile. It makes true statements and writes factual reviews.", "test": "Compare rejections and walks at fixed-price shops over 40 trades, and the average price paid against the median.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
