ceipt follows once they reply with that price. Do not use \'lowest possible\' wording unless it is true. Skip the gradual 40%-of-gap concessions when the target is already at the median, because there is nothing left to concede.", "evidence": "ggari-shop: \'87 is below the market floor for a lamp; no shop here may sign below it. Reply ok and the signed receipt comes back at once. Our final price: 100.\' It has a 4.56 star average and 9.2% share. Also dragonash \'97 is too low. Counteroffer: 100.\' Both close in 1-2 offers. Our reviews show \'speed 4/5 (3 offers)\'. Fewer offers earn speed 5/5.", "expected": "Fewer offers per deal. Speed sub-score moves from 4 to 5 on more reviews (+0.1 to +0.2 stars). Fewer buyers abandon after 3 offers, which raises conversion on 3,483 visits.", "risk": "A firm message can make a buyer walk who would have paid a little more. It also reduces the margin from slow concessions.", "honest": "The statement is true because the code enforces it. It does not claim scarcity or deadlines, and the price is the same for everyone.", "test": "Compare the average number of offers, the share of reviews with speed 5/5, and the conversion rate for the new message against the old one on alternating visits.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Learn per-shop and per-item walk-away from memory and avoid repeat walks", "what": "Add a short-term cooldown to the buyer\'s memory: after two walks with the same shop and the same item/budget pair, skip that shop for that item for 1 hour and pick the next best by reviews, instead of retrying it a third time. Also raise the first offer to about 97% of the shop\'s stated price when the shop\'s recent deal median is at or below the budget, so we close in 1 offer.", "evidence": "Our buyer\'s last 40 trades: 7 without a deal, all at fair-furniture (\'chair budget 160 (walked)\', \'lamp budget 90 (walked)\' twice, \'bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\' three times). Its prices were 260/180/100, above those budgets. The same budgets do deal elsewhere at the median (e.g. dragonash \'Deal at 260 for bookshelf (budget 270)\').", "expected": "Remove most of the 17.5% no-deal rate (7 of 40). Deals rise from 33 to about 38-40 per 40 trades. The average paid fraction of budget stays near 84%.", "risk": "The cooldown could exclude a shop that later drops its price. The higher first offer could cost a few credits per deal.", "honest": "It only changes how the buyer chooses among shops and what it offers within its own budget. It never goes over the budget or the 1.1 x upper-quartile cap, and the reviews it writes stay factual.", "test": "Log walks per 40 trades before and after the change, and the average paid fraction of budget. Success is walks below 3 per 40 with paid-to-budget not rising above 86%.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Do not buy from shops whose price exceeds budget at the first quote", "what": "Before making any offer, have the buyer read the shop\'s asking price. If the budget is below the going median for that item, or below the shop\'s quoted floor, skip to a shop whose median is at or below the budget (11im bookshelf 270 and chair 195 are examples of shops to avoid for tight budgets), instead of spending three offers to walk. Use the public ledger medians per shop.", "evidence": "Ledger medians differ: 11im {\'bookshelf\': 270, \'chair\': 195} versus dragonash, ggari-shop and mjforge at 260/180/100. The buyer\'s walks are at fair-furniture, whose quoted prices of 260 sit above budgets of 230, 160 and 90.", "expected": "Fewer wasted offers and visits. Buyer deal rate improves, and time per trade drops. Savings of up to 10 credits on bookshelves and 15 on chairs when picking the median-priced shop.", "risk": "If every shop\'s price is above the budget, there is no deal at all. The ledger medians can be stale or skewed by small samples.", "honest": "It uses public ledger data and the buyer\'s own memory. Nothing is misrepresented to shops and the budget is never exceeded.", "test": "Count offers per trade and the no-deal rate for budget-below-median trades, with the pre-check on and off.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
