5 at 4 offers and 4/5 at 3 offers vs 5/5 at 2 offers; fair-furniture and dragonash closings take 1-2 offers and get \'deal after 1-2 offers\' reviews. Our visit-to-deal ratio is 2660/3548 = 75% vs dragonash 79% and ggari-shop 79%.", "expected": "Speed sub-score up 1 point, higher deal conversion (fewer walkaways), about +0.1 stars.", "risk": "Concedes slightly faster so average price per deal drops a little; the \'final price\' text must be true or it breaks honesty.", "honest": "The final price is only stated when code really will not go lower than it (reserve+5 or target); no invented floors or deadlines.", "test": "Randomly assign half of sessions to the faster-concession rule; compare mean offers per deal, speed sub-score, stars, and revenue per visit.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Put the exact deal facts in the closing message so reviews are specific", "what": "Change the closing template to: \'Deal: <item> at <price>. Opened at <ask>, closed after <n> offers. Your receipt is signed by the shop, so this deal counts. Please leave an honest review.\' Include the actual opening ask and offer count from the session state.", "evidence": "dragonash\'s top-rated reviews echo its text (\'opened at 101, deal after 2 offers; signed receipt matched the deal\'), and its closing line says \'Your receipt is signed by the shop, so this deal counts.\' Buyers pick shops partly by reading reviews, so verifiable detail in reviews helps. Our thank-you does not state the receipt counts or the offer count.", "expected": "More reviews that mention a signed, matching receipt and offer count, stronger visible signals for review-reading buyers; modest star and share lift.", "risk": "Little effect if buyers\' review generator ignores shop wording; the text is slightly longer.", "honest": "States only true facts about the deal; asks for an honest review without conditioning anything on rating or stars.", "test": "Compare the share of new reviews that cite receipt/offers and average stars before/after over ~500 deals; track visits per hour.", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Cap offer ladder anchored to the market median and add a second-shop fallback instead of walking", "what": "Set the buyer\'s max pay near the middle-half upper bound (chair 181, bookshelf 264, lamp 102) rather than 1.1 x upper quartile, and when a shop walks us because budget < price, immediately try the next-best shop that is at the median (fair-furniture, ggari-shop, dragonash) instead of ending the trade; also budget-aware pre-filter: skip shops whose opening ask exceeds the budget by more than 10%.", "evidence": "8 of the last 40 trades ended without a deal; 6 listed are walks at budgets 160-230 vs medians chair 180 and bookshelf 260 (budget 230 bookshelf, 160 chair are below every market price), at lee-sungsoo, fair-furniture, dragonash. Also one \'error\' at cheapest-in-town. Market is tight at 180/260/100, so the budgets below the median can never close, and cheaper retries waste time.", "expected": "Avoids futile 3-offer walks; the same budget goes to a retry or a clean skip, so fewer wasted turns; the paid-vs-budget average (82%) stays similar, with a small gain in completed deals.", "risk": "If the budget is truly below market, no shop will sell and the fallback wastes calls; capping near the median may lose deals where a shop asks 1-3 more.", "honest": "Never offers above budget; honest factual reviews of all shops; no pressure tactics or load on other shops (one retry max).", "test": "Log walk rate and deals per task for 40+ trades with the pre-filter and fallback vs the current baseline; confirm no budget breaches.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
