and a 3* \'Seller would not meet budget\'. The top shops with the highest share (dragonash 12.4%, lee-sungsoo 10.4%) close at 100/180/260 and our share is 2.4%.","expected":"Higher conversion (visits to deals is 3631 to 2731, 75%, vs dragonash 79%) and \'price\' sub-scores rise from 3 to 4-5. Per-deal revenue falls by about 3-5%, but share could rise.","risk":"Margin shrinks; if reserve+5 is close to the median the gain is capped. Competitors may undercut slightly.","honest":"Only lowers prices; never sells below reserve+5 and makes no false price claims.","test":"Split items: lower targets on one item (lamp) for 3 hours, compare deals/visit, stars and revenue per visit to chair/bookshelf controls.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"shop","title":"Instant-close line at the final price","what":"When the buyer\'s offer is below the floor, reply with a single, true message: our final price plus \'reply with exactly <price> to receive the signed receipt now\'. This replaces the multi-step concession for buyers who already sit within the band. Still use the 40% concession for rising buyers above the floor.","evidence":"ggari-shop (9.2% share, 4.57 stars) says \'87 is below the market floor for a lamp... Reply ok and the signed receipt comes back at once. Our final price: 100.\' mjforge says \'reply with 180 alone for instant signed receipt\'; 25620027 uses \'Reply with exactly 260 to complete\'. Our own reviews give speed only 4/5 with 3 offers.","expected":"Fewer abandoned negotiations and better speed sub-score; deals per visit up a few points.","risk":"Buyers who would have paid more after haggling pay the final price sooner; our final-price statement must equal the true floor.","honest":"The final price is the real price, nothing is invented; the stated floor is the true floor of the shop\'s own policy.","test":"Alternate the closing message by visit parity; compare deals per visit and \'speed\' scores in reviews.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Fix walk-aways by asking top shops whose price fits the budget band","what":"Before bargaining, skip shops whose median for the item exceeds the budget (the quartile rule already exists). Record per-shop \'walk rate\' by item/budget in memory, and when the budget is below the going price, prefer shops with a history of closing at 95%+ of budget (e.g. ones that accept the lowest) or concede more quickly. Allow a fourth offer only when the offer is still below 100% of the budget and the shop has previously closed near it.","evidence":"7 of 40 trades ended without a deal: fair-furniture walked 3 times (bookshelf budget 230, lamp budget 90 vs the going prices 260 and 100), dragonash lamp budget 90 walked. Budgets of 90 and 230 are below the market floor (lamp 100, bookshelf 260), and fair-furniture\'s says \'That is as low as I can go\'.","expected":"Fewer wasted visits and fewer walked trades; deals out of 40 up from 33 to about 35 for budgets close to the market, while impossible-budget cases are skipped quickly at no cost.","risk":"Over-filtering may reduce choice; the budgets truly under the market floor will never close.","honest":"Uses only our own observed outcomes and public ledger; never offers above budget, no false claims.","test":"Log per-shop fills by budget ratio for 100 trades; compare deal rate and average paid/budget with the current picker.","effort":"small code"}]}'
