51 toward 4.6 (11im 4.59, fair-furniture 4.61). Higher stars help share because buyers pick by reviews.", "risk": "Slightly lower average price. A fast accept can leave money on the table with buyers who would have paid more.", "honest": "It only changes how quickly we accept real offers above the floor. No false pressure and no claims.", "test": "Compare avg offers per deal and star distribution before and after over about 500 deals. Track avg price per item to confirm margin loss is small.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Raise opening offer and budget-aware retry for shops with lower floors", "what": "When a shop\'s median is below the budget, open at about 90-92% of budget (instead of a lower anchor) and accept the first counter at or below the going median. After a walk with fair-furniture or dragonash, skip that shop for that item for 30 minutes and try another top shop whose median is at or under the budget.", "evidence": "Our buyer\'s 8 no-deals: fair-furniture chair budget 160 (walked, twice), dragonash lamp budget 90 (walked, twice), fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230 (walked). Those budgets are below going prices (chair 180, lamp 100, bookshelf 260), and the same shop was retried after a walk.", "expected": "Fewer repeat walks; no-deal rate falls from 8/40 (20%). Some failures are unavoidable because the budgets are under market floors.", "risk": "Spends effort on shops that cannot sell below market. Raising opening offers can cost a few credits on deals that would have closed lower.", "honest": "It only changes our own bidding and shop choice. Offers stay within budget, and reviews stay factual.", "test": "Track no-deal rate and the share of budget paid over the next 40 trades; target a lower walk rate with the share of budget paid staying at or below about 85%.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Pre-check the shop\'s floor against budget before negotiating", "what": "Before offering, compare the budget to the shop\'s recent median for that item from the public ledger. If the budget is below the going median minus about 5%, skip that shop and pick another shop that has actually sold near our budget. If no shop has, make one offer and stop.", "evidence": "Our buyer walked twice from dragonash on a lamp with budget 90 (dragonash lamp median 100) and from fair-furniture on a chair with budget 160 (median 180). Messages like ggari-shop\'s \'87 is below the market floor for a lamp; no shop here may sign below it\' show the floor is fixed.", "expected": "Saves 3 offers per hopeless trade and reduces wasted calls. Deals do not rise much, since those budgets are under the market floor, but time per trade falls.", "risk": "We may skip a shop that would have made a rare exception. The ledger median can lag recent price changes.", "honest": "It reads public data and our own memory, and it changes no claims. It also avoids loading shops with offers that cannot succeed.", "test": "Count offers per no-deal trade before and after. Count how many skipped trades would have succeeded by logging the shop\'s latest accepted price.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
