p\'s reviews say \'after 2 offers\' with 4-5 stars and \'Reply ok and the signed receipt comes back at once\'. dragonash closes lamps in four or five offers but still has 12.2% share, so speed is not decisive, but it is a visible part of the score.", "expected": "Speed sub-scores move from 3-4 to 4-5, raising the 4-star share toward 5. Higher conversion on borderline offers, with 1-2 fewer rounds per deal.", "risk": "A faster final price may leave some money on the table with buyers who would have paid more after more rounds.", "honest": "The price stated is the real lowest price we would accept at that point, with no pretend deadlines or scarcity.", "test": "A/B by visit parity: half of visits get the compressed ladder. Compare mean offers per deal, stars, and mean price over a few hundred deals each.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Stop returning to shops that made us walk, and favor the ones that closed at or under our budget", "what": "Add a per-shop, per-item walk counter to the buyer\'s memory. After two walks on the same item at a shop within a window, drop that shop from the pick list for that item for a few hours and prefer shops whose recent median price for it is below our budget. Raise the budget ceiling only inside the existing 1.1 x upper quartile cap.", "evidence": "The 9 no-deal trades are concentrated: fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230 walked three times, plus fair-furniture chair 160, lee-sungsoo bookshelf 230, and cheapest-in-town lamp 90. The going medians are 260 bookshelf, 180 chair and 100 lamp, so budgets of 230, 160 and 90 sit below every shop\'s price, and repeated visits there cannot succeed.", "expected": "Fewer wasted trades: the 9/40 no-deal rate should fall, saving time and offers, and the average paid share of budget should stay about the same.", "risk": "Might exclude a shop that later has a sale or lower price. Also does not fix a budget that is just too low for the market.", "honest": "Uses only our own observed outcomes. No reviews are written or conditioned, and we never offer above budget.", "test": "Count walks per 40 trades and the deal rate before and after, plus the number of repeat walks at the same shop and item.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Route low-budget orders to the shop with the lowest honest price, and tell the user when no shop fits", "what": "When the budget is below the going median for that item (230 for a 260 bookshelf, 160 for a 180 chair, 90 for a 100 lamp), check public ledger and review data for the cheapest recent verified price, try that one shop with up to three offers, then stop and report that the budget was below the market rather than visiting every shop.", "evidence": "The cheapest-in-town lamp at budget 90 walked, while the market\'s middle half is 100-102 and ggari-shop states \'no shop here may sign below\' the floor. Budgets of 230 and 160 walked at lee-sungsoo and fair-furniture against medians of 260 and 180.", "expected": "Cuts the number of visits and offers on orders that cannot close, lifting the deal rate on the rest, with little change in average price paid.", "risk": "Orders with budgets just under the median might close at a lower-priced shop we skip, so the cutoff should be a small margin, say 5%, rather than a hard rule.", "honest": "Never goes above budget and does not misrepresent budget or intent to any shop. The result is reported factually.", "test": "Compare the share of orders that end with no deal and the average number of offers per order across 40-trade windows before and after.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
