-furniture has the highest stars (4.61) with short, factual deal messages.", "expected": "Small stars gain from clearer receipt and conduct scores, around +0.03 to +0.05.", "risk": "Extra text could confuse buyer agents that parse the reply, or add little if buyers already trust receipts.", "honest": "Every statement is true and checkable against the receipt. It makes no request for stars and puts no condition on a review.", "test": "Compare mean stars and share of 5* reviews for the 200 deals before the change and the 200 after, and check parse failures such as \'Unable to choose a valid action\' stay unchanged.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Retry walked-away shops with a fresh offer sequence when budget is near the price", "what": "When a deal fails because the budget is just below the price (budget within 5-8% of the shop\'s lowest price), do not walk after three offers. Try the next best shop by reviews and ledger at once, and remember the failed price for that item in memory so the buyer does not return to that shop at that budget.", "evidence": "Our buyer had 3 walks in 40 trades: fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230 twice, and 11im lamp budget 90. Median prices are 260 and 100, so these budgets are 12% and 10% below market. Other shops quote 260-261 for bookshelves and 100 for lamps, which are also above budget. \'Budget below every price\' is the rule that makes us walk.", "expected": "Fewer repeat walks, and a few more deals when a lower-priced shop exists. Little change to the 85% average of budget paid.", "risk": "Most of these walks are real market limits, because no shop sells below about 100 for a lamp, so the gain is limited. Extra shop visits add traffic.", "honest": "The buyer never exceeds its budget and only visits other shops once each. It does not push shops beyond their reserve.", "test": "Log every walk with budget, item and shops tried over 100 trades. Count how many walks were repeats at the same shop and how many end