e (\'Counteroffer: 106\') and willow-goods (\'Counteroffer: 198\'), show different counters. 35 of 40 trades closed, so about 12% are lost.", "expected": "Deal rate may go up a little, from 87.5%. Where the budget is below the going price, a second shop might still find a lower counter. The average paid share (82.6%) stays the same or drops slightly.", "risk": "If the budget really is below the market, the extra shop gives nothing and costs time and visits. More visits may also slightly raise load on other shops.", "honest": "It is ordinary comparison shopping. It makes no false claims and never offers above the budget, and it does not overload anyone because it adds at most one extra negotiation per task.", "test": "For tasks where the first shop is walked, count how many are completed at the second shop, and the price paid compared with the budget. Compare the overall deal rate with the earlier 35/40 over 100 or more trades.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Avoid shops with low review stars and shops with a high walk rate", "what": "Add a walk-rate term to the buyer\'s shop score: for each shop, track walks divided by visits from the buyer\'s own memory. Lower the rank of shops that walked it two or more times in the last 10 visits for the same item class. Use the quality of the review text (a factual price, a matched receipt) in the ranking, and put little weight on the star average.", "evidence": "All 5 walks in the buyer\'s last 40 trades are lee-sungsoo (3) and fair-furniture (2). Both shops have big shares (9.1% and 8.1%), so the current ranking keeps choosing them. lee-sungsoo has a 4.35 average with a 3* review (\'the quality didn\'t impress me\'). Its stamp message demands 4 different codes for its lowest price, which a one-off buyer cannot meet. JangSeongWoong (4.03 stars) and 11im, by contrast, closed at the median.", "expected": "Fewer wasted visits and a higher deal rate. Cheaper closes at shops that hold near the median, like gga