he deal\', \'receipt matches item and task\'). cheapest-in-town gets 4* with \'zero added fees; your signed receipt records this exact item, price and task\' and is at 5.6% share on about 60% of the deals dragonash gets. The 2* for fair-furniture complains about quality not being worth the price, so the product description needs to be accurate rather than inflated.", "expected": "Small stars lift (about +0.05) and better review text that buyers\' memory-based pickers can use; no change to price.", "risk": "Longer text could confuse parsers that look for the number, so keep the price as a plain number in a fixed position. A catalog detail that is untrue would break honesty.", "honest": "Describes only true features of the receipt and of items we really have; no invented provenance (cheapest-in-town\'s \'fictional collection\' is a pattern we do not copy).", "test": "Randomise the message template 50/50 per deal and compare mean stars and review-length/receipt-mention rate over a few hundred deals each.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Stop walking on budgets below the going price; pre-screen and route to shops that can fill them", "what": "Before the first offer, compare the task budget with the current median for that item. If the budget is below the lowest ask in the middle half (lamp budget 90 versus 100, chair 160 versus 180, bookshelf 230 versus 260), do not spend three offers on shops that will refuse; skip the shop quickly, and record \'budget below market\' in memory so the visit is not counted as a shop failure and does not hurt that shop\'s record. Where the budget is within about 3% of the floor, go straight to the lowest-price shop with a single offer at budget.", "evidence": "Our 8 unfilled trades are all low budgets: lee-sungsoo lamp 90 (walked) three times, dragonash lamp 90, fair-furniture lamp 90, 25620027 chair 160, lee-sungsoo bookshelf 230. Those shops\' floors are 100/180/260 (\'This is our floor, we never sell below it: 100