eapest-in-town says \'reply with 180 alone for instant signed receipt\' and buyer review \'Signed receipt matched. 1 offer until the end\'. Our visits-to-deals ratio is 3350/4391 = 76%, versus 11im\'s 67%, dragonash\'s 78% and cheapest-in-town\'s 74%, so there is no conversion edge.", "expected": "Higher speed sub-scores and conversion, with 4-5 star reviews and a small share gain. Average price per deal drops slightly.", "risk": "We may leave money on the table when buyers would have paid more, and the fast path could be used by buyers who offer exactly the floor.", "honest": "We state the actual price and accept it, and the receipt stays signed. We make no urgency claims.", "test": "A/B by item: fast-accept on chairs for 3 hours against the current logic on lamps. Compare offers per deal, deals per visit and stars.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Avoid and rank shops by observed first-counter price", "what": "Add to the buyer\'s memory a per-shop record of the first counter and final price paid per item. Open at the shop\'s lowest recent first-counter instead of a fixed fraction of budget, and prefer shops where the final price is at or near median with the fewest offers. Skip shops whose counters show a hard floor above our budget before spending three offers.", "evidence": "Our buyer walked in 8 of 40 trades, mostly \'walked\' on chairs with budget 160 at 25620027, fair-furniture and ggari-shop, which all sell at 180, and on lamp budget 90 at Kiro729 and lee-sungsoo with median 100. We spent offers on shops that could not meet a budget below the median.", "expected": "Fewer wasted trades and lower time per task. The deal rate over the last 40 trades should rise from 80% (32/40), and average paid share of budget should stay at or below 85%.", "risk": "A stale memory could exclude a shop that later lowers its price. The buyer may also get fewer candidate shops.", "honest": "It uses only our own past observations and public prices, and it only pays within budget.", "test": "Log walk-aways by cause for 40 trades before and after, and compare deal rate and percentage of budget paid.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Pre-filter by budget against the going median", "what": "When the budget is below the item\'s going median (lamp budget 90, chair budget 160), do not make three futile offers to shops. Make a single offer to the 2-3 shops whose recent medians are lowest (e.g. yms979 floor 100 does not qualify, so check cheapest-in-town, which accepts the lowest price subject to a minimum), and record the outcome as \'no deal, budget under market\' in a factual review, rather than a general complaint about the shop.", "evidence": "Walk-aways at budget 90 for lamp (Kiro729, lee-sungsoo) and budget 160 for chair (25620027, fair-furniture, ggari-shop), while the going medians are lamp 100 and chair 180. The myeonggi bookshelf at budget 230 was rejected, against a median of 260. cheapest-in-town\'s \'we accept your desired price or lower, subject to our minimum selling price\' is the only observed hint of flexibility.", "expected": "Fewer offers per walk-away, and factual no-deal reviews that do not mislead other buyers about shops that were fairly priced. Little effect on deal count, since those budgets are below market.", "risk": "A single offer to a handful of shops may miss an occasional low price from a shop that can actually sell below the median.", "honest": "Reviews remain factual about why there was no deal (budget under the going median), and the budget is never exceeded.", "test": "Count offers spent on trades with budget below median over 40 trades, and compare it with the 8-walk baseline.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
