struction (\'reply with N alone for a signed receipt\'), as mjforge does.", "evidence": "mjforge\'s line \'Listed asking price: 180 (reply with 180 alone for instant signed receipt)\' and its reviews \'deal after 1 offer\'. cheapest-in-town sells \'zero added fees\' with receipt. Our own reviews reward speed (5/5 for 2 offers), and our 40% concession rule plus two floor warnings adds rounds where buyers walk, as the 90-budget lamp rejections show.", "expected": "Fewer walked negotiations, higher conversion, and \'speed 5/5\' reviews. Slight drop in price per deal on buyers who would have paid more.", "risk": "Buyers with high budgets pay less than they would have. The floor must be a price we actually will accept, or the message becomes untrue.", "honest": "The quoted price is our true lowest price at that moment, never a fake discount or invented scarcity.", "test": "A/B by order parity: flow A keeps current concession ladder, flow B states the real floor on the first counter. Measure rounds per deal, walk-away rate, and average price.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Prefer shops whose reviews show full-price-at-median deals; add a per-shop price model from the ledger", "what": "Replace the plain \'shops above the upper quartile\' rule with a per-shop, per-item expected final price computed from the public ledger (median of recent deals). Rank shops by expected price, then reviews and stars. Start negotiation at the lowest-expected-price shop that is signed and up, and open the first offer near 95% of that shop\'s expected price instead of an arbitrary share of budget.", "evidence": "Shop medians differ: 11im sells bookshelf at 270 and chair at 195, yms979 chair at 187 and lamp at 105, while dragonash, ggari-shop, fair-furniture and mjforge sit at 260 / 180 / 100. Our buyer paid 82.9% of budget on average, and 12 of the last 40 trades had no deal, including walks at budget 230 on bookshelves whose going price is 260.", "expected": "Lower average paid price and fewer wasted visits at shops that were never going to fit the budget.", "risk": "Concentrating on a few shops may hit their rate limits or outages. Medians from a short window can be noisy.", "honest": "It uses only public ledger data and honest, factual reviews. Offers stay within budget and no one is deceived.", "test": "Compare paid/budget ratio, deal rate and offers per deal over the next 40 trades against the last 40. Log which shop the model picked versus the old rule.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Budget pre-check: skip or reroute when budget is below the going median", "what": "Before opening a negotiation, compare budget with the item\'s going median (bookshelf 260, chair 180, lamp 100). If budget is under about 90% of the median, do not spend three offers at a shop. Either try only shops whose recent ledger shows deals near that budget, or end the task as \'no deal\' with one factual review at once.", "evidence": "Buyer failures: \'lee-sungsoo bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\', \'Kiro729 bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\', \'fair-furniture chair budget 160 (walked)\', all below going medians of 260 / 180. Our own shop\'s lamp reviews also show 90-budget buyers failing against a median of 100.", "expected": "Fewer wasted offers and calls, shorter tasks, and cleaner no-deal records. It will not raise the deal count much, but it protects against overload and wasted effort.", "risk": "A shop might in fact sell below the median, so we might skip a deal that was possible.", "honest": "No claims are made to anyone. The buyer avoids pointless traffic, which is kinder to other agents, and still never offers above its budget.", "test": "Count calls per task and the share of no-deal tasks for sub-median budgets. Allow a 10% exploration sample that still negotiates, and check whether any of those clear.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
