rice paid. Most of the 12 will still fail.","risk":"Extra visits add load to other shops and take time; if budget really is below all floors, it is wasted effort; note a ggari-shop REJECTED state, which suggests an error to inspect.","honest":"Real offers within our own budget; no deception; at most a few offers per shop, so no overloading.","test":"Count deals per 40 trades and the number of shop attempts per trade, before versus after, split by budget-to-median ratio (under 0.9, 0.9-1.0, above 1.0).","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Raise the concession per offer when budget is at least the median","what":"Our buyer raises at most 40% of the gap per offer, so it needs many rounds. When budget >= going median, open at about 90% of the median and then raise 60% of the gap on the first two offers, then 40%, and never above budget or 1.1 x upper quartile. The target is to reach 180/260/100 in 2-3 offers instead of 7-8.","evidence":"fair-furniture \'deal after 8 offers\' and cheapest-in-town \'deal after 7 offers\' on prices that equal the median anyway; shops state a fixed \'final price\' (ggari-shop: \'Our final price: 180\'; yms979 \'This is our floor... 100\'), so slow raising adds rounds without lowering the final price. Our buyer pays 84.2% of budget on average.","expected":"Faster trades, same price (shops hold floors), more deals completed before the three-offer walk limit; price paid is similar or slightly higher on rare shops that would have conceded.","risk":"Where a shop would really have conceded below the median, we pay a bit more; the 11im/yms979 shops with higher medians (195, 187, 270) make that possible.","honest":"Offers stay within budget and within the 1.1 x upper quartile cap; the buyer\'s reviews stay factual.","test":"Compare offers per deal, deal rate, and paid-to-median ratio over 40-trade windows, by shop.","effort":"knob"}]}'
