w the 2* \'refused to negotiate reasonably\' and a no-receipt 3* after 4 rounds.", "expected": "Fewer walked/aborted negotiations, higher conversion (visits 4988 vs deals 3827), better stars through fewer frustrating long haggles.", "risk": "Buyers who would have raised their offer stop at the fast price; a small revenue loss per deal.", "honest": "States a true price and a real instant-receipt behaviour; no deadline or scarcity pressure.", "test": "Compare deals/visits and mean offers-to-close before and after for 12h; track share of 3* and below that mention negotiation.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Put the quote and a verifiable receipt line in every deal message", "what": "Make deal message state item, final price, \'no added fees\', and \'signed receipt records this exact item, price and task\', and avoid the pending-signature wording. Check that the receipt is delivered in the same message as the deal acceptance, since one review mentions no receipt and another \'market signature validation pending\'.", "evidence": "Our reviews: \'there was no receipt to confirm a deal\' and \'market signature validation pending\'. Leaders\' reviews say \'receipt matched\' and cheapest-in-town/psyoung82/25620027 messages say \'signed receipt records this exact item, price and task\'. Round 2 counts only signed receipts.", "expected": "Fewer lost receipts counted in Round 2, fewer 3* reviews on receipt issues, slightly higher stars (4.5 vs 4.57-4.59 leaders).", "risk": "Longer message with a small 8B model output; template text could be cut off. Mitigate by composing it in code, not the LLM.", "honest": "Describes behaviour we actually perform; review request stays neutral (\'honest review\').", "test": "Count receipts with order vs deals (now 3827/3827) and the share of reviews that mention receipts; check no-receipt cases in logs per 1000 deals.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Walk-away anchoring on the going price, not the budget", "what": "Open at about the lower quartile of the market (e.g. lamp 95-100, chair 175-180, bookshelf 255-260) and raise by at most 40% of the gap, but cap the final price at the market median (+ small margin) rather than 1.1 x upper quartile. Prefer shops whose public medians equal the market median and skip shops with a higher median (11im 270/195, yms979 187/270).", "evidence": "Buyer paid 89.6% of budget on average over 27 deals while the top shops sell at 260/180/100 even for budgets of 295-360 (\'Deal at 260 ... budget 295\', \'Paid 260.0 of 325\'). 11im and yms979 show medians above market (bookshelf 270, chair 195/187). Many of our 13 no-deals are lamp budget 90 against a market of 100, so route those to cheaper shops or skip rather than haggle.", "expected": "Lower average spend per deal (toward ~80% of budget), fewer wasted rejected/walked attempts by pre-filtering items where budget < median.", "risk": "Capping at median may cause more no-deals with shops that hold at 265-270; some tasks go unfilled.", "honest": "Buyer bids only within its own budget and uses public ledger data; reviews stay factual.", "test": "Compare mean paid/budget and deal rate over the next 40 trades against the last 40 (89.6%, 27/40).", "effort": "small code"}]}'
