jforge: \'Reply with 260 alone for instant signed receipt\'. Our 3* and 4* reviews mention 2-3 rounds for chair at 183-185. fair-furniture needed 5-7 offers and stars of 4.59 depend on a smooth close; ggari-shop\'s \'close\' message with counteroffer 182 closes at 180.", "expected": "Fewer buyer walk-aways after 3 offers (buyers give up after three offers), deals per visit up a couple of points, small price drop.", "risk": "Buyers who would have paid more get a quick close at the final price; the shorter negotiation may reduce price discovery.", "honest": "The quoted price is a real price we will charge, never below reserve+5, with no invented deadline or scarcity.", "test": "Log offers-to-close and walk rate for buyers in the near-target band before and after for one hour windows; compare to a control share of traffic.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Prefer shops whose medians are at the going price and skip those with higher own medians", "what": "Rank shops by the median per item reported in the public ledger and drop any whose median exceeds the going median by more than one credit (11im bookshelf 270, chair 195; yms979 chair 187, bookshelf 270) when a shop at the going median is available. Use one shop at the going median first, then fall back.", "evidence": "Top shops table: dragonash, ggari-shop, lee-sungsoo, mjforge, fair-furniture and cheapest-in-town all have medians 260/180/100; 11im has chair 195 and bookshelf 270; yms979 has chair 187, bookshelf 270. Our buyer paid 85.4% of budget on average and had 13 of 40 trades without a deal.", "expected": "Average price paid down 1-4% on chairs and bookshelves, and fewer walk-aways because opening asks are closer to what budgets cover.", "risk": "Concentrating on a few shops may raise load on them and slow responses; ledger medians lag. Our buyer already skips shops above the upper quartile, so the extra gain is small.", "honest": "Uses only public ledger data and our own memory; no manipulation of others.", "test": "Compare paid/budget ratio and no-deal rate over the next 40 trades vs the last 40, split by shop median tier.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the going median and make a single jump to the shop\'s counter instead of three small raises", "what": "Start the first offer at the going median (e.g. chair 180, lamp 100, bookshelf 260) when the budget covers it, and if the shop counters with a price at or below 1.02x median, accept immediately. Reserve the 40%-of-gap raises for shops that open above the upper quartile. For tasks where budget is below the median (chair budget 160, bookshelf 230, lamp 90), do not spend three offers on shops that quote 180-182 on a 160 budget; walk after one offer and try a different shop.", "evidence": "Shops\' says: ggari-shop \'176 is too low. Counteroffer: 182\' and \'180 is close. Counteroffer: 182\'; dragonash \'215 is too low. Counteroffer: 260\'. Our no-deal list: fair-furniture chair budget 160 (walked), ggari-shop chair budget 160 (walked), fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230 (walked), dragonash lamp budget 90 (walked); going prices show chair 180, bookshelf 260, lamp 100 are all above those budgets.", "expected": "Fewer wasted visits on infeasible budgets, so more tries on shops that can sell near budget (e.g. ones with promotional or loyalty discounts); deal rate up and time per task down.", "risk": "Opening at the median forgoes the 1-2% that a lower start sometimes wins, and walking early could skip a shop that would have discounted.", "honest": "Offers never exceed the budget and reviews stay factual; no claim about other offers is made to shops.", "test": "Track deals per visit and paid/budget over the next 40 trades; count offers per deal and the share of infeasible-budget tasks that end in one-offer walks.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
