p the floor at reserve + 5 and the 40% concession rule. Run this as a per-item knob in meta_shop.py for bookshelves only.", "evidence": "Our reviews: \'bookshelf at 270 | price 4/5 (median 273)\' and \'bookshelf at 265 | price 4/5 (median 273)\', plus the 3* no-deal at 275->265 where the floor was above the buyer\'s limit. Market: bookshelf median 260, middle half 260-268. dragonash, ggari-shop, lee-sungsoo, mjforge, cheapest-in-town and 25620027 all sit at 260. Our buyers pay 265-270, which is 5-10 above the market, and the price sub-score drops to 4/5.", "expected": "Price score 4 -> 5 on bookshelves, fewer no-deals, share up. Revenue per bookshelf falls 5-10 (2-4%), but more deals and better reviews should outweigh it, provided the 260 price stays above reserve + 5.", "risk": "If reserve + 5 is close to 260, margin per deal shrinks. Buyers with higher budgets would have paid more.", "honest": "Prices stay above reserve + 5, and no claim about \'was\' prices is made. We simply price at the market median.", "test": "Split bookshelf traffic 50/50 between the old and new target for 6h. Compare deal rate, average price and stars per bookshelf visit.", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Raise the budget-below-price walk rule into a per-shop price memory", "what": "Record for each shop and item the lowest price at which we ever closed, and the floor it showed when we walked. Before a visit, skip shops whose last known floor is above our budget for that item. Re-probe such a shop at most once an hour, so the memory does not go stale.", "evidence": "Our buyer: 9 of the last 40 trades had no deal. Six are listed, and most are walks where the budget was below the shop\'s price: dragonash lamp budget 90 (walked, twice), ggari-shop lamp budget 90 (walked), lee-sungsoo chair budget 160 and bookshelf 230 (walked). These shops show a lamp floor of 100, a chair floor of 180 and a bookshelf floor of 260. One more is an error: cheapest-in-town bookshelf budget 295.", "e