l price: 260\' and mjforge: \'Reply with 260 alone for instant signed receipt.\' Our own reviews give speed 3/5 for 4 offers and 4/5 for 3 offers. Leaders get \'deal after 3 offers\' and fast checkout.", "expected": "Speed sub-score of 4-5/5 and a gain in stars of about 0.1. Shorter negotiations lower the chance the buyer\'s three-offer limit ends the deal.", "risk": "A firm price shown too early may lose buyers who would have paid more under the ladder. Saying \'no shop may sign below it\' would be false for us, so we must not copy that wording.", "honest": "States only our true final price and says nothing about other shops, scarcity or deadlines.", "test": "Compare average offers per deal, speed sub-score and deal rate for 100 deals on each variant.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Reserve a cap of upper-quartile for stable asks, and skip shops that repeat a high flat ask", "what": "Add a per-shop, per-item memory of the first-ask-to-final ratio. If a shop\'s asks have stayed flat above the market median (like psyoung82 at 270 for bookshelf) after two offers, stop raising and walk to the next shop. Cap the payment at the lower edge of the middle half plus 2% for bookshelf, chair and lamp, not 1.1 x the upper quartile.", "evidence": "Our buyer paid 270 twice at psyoung82 with asks [270 x8] and offers rising 255 to 270, while \'it sold at 260 to others\'. The middle half is 260-265, so 1.1 x upper quartile allows about 291 and is far above the real going price. 29 of 40 trades closed at 82.9% of budget.", "expected": "Saves about 10 credits per overpaid trade, around 4%, at the price of a slightly higher walk rate. Average share of budget paid should fall below 80%.", "risk": "A tighter cap may leave some orders unfilled. Prices may rise later, so the cap must follow the rolling quartiles.", "honest": "Only changes how much our buyer pays. Reviews we write stay factual, and nothing is bought or conditioned.", "test": "Log price paid minus median per deal, and walk rate, for 40 trades before and after.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Retry a walked order at a different shop within budget", "what": "When the three-offer walk ends with no deal because the budget is below the ask, do not log it as lost. Move to the next-best shop by reviews and ledger whose ledger median is within budget, using the same budget and the same cap. Do this at most twice per order.", "evidence": "11 trades without a deal, including repeated lee-sungsoo lamp budget 90 (walked) four times and chair budget 160 (walked), and nocha12 bookshelf budget 230 (walked). Medians are 100 for lamp, 180 for chair and 260 for bookshelf, so these budgets are below every price. Retrying only at lee-sungsoo wastes calls.", "expected": "Does not make impossible orders succeed. It stops us returning to the same shop that cannot meet the budget, and cuts wasted offers. If any shop\'s floor is under budget, deals rise slightly.", "risk": "Orders whose budget is below every floor will still fail. Extra calls may add load on small shops, so keep the cap at two.", "honest": "Offers stay within the buyer\'s budget, no shop is overloaded, and no reviews are conditioned.", "test": "Count the share of orders converted after a first walk and the extra offers per order, for 50 orders.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
