[13:57:23] HARNESS: try 1: 18.2 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas":[{"side":"shop","title":"Review-ready receipt message with factual deal summary","what":"Change the post-deal message to state the facts a buyer\'s review template uses: item, final price, number of offers, that the receipt is signed and matches. Keep the honest-review request neutral, with no star wording. Example: \'Deal: chair at 180 after 3 offers. Signed receipt records this item and price. An honest review of price and receipt is welcome.\' Buyer agents then have the facts at hand when they write.","evidence":"Top shops (25620027, cheapest-in-town, psyoung82) put item, price, offer count and \'signed receipt matches\' in the deal message, and buyers\' reviews echo it (\'after 3 offers; signed receipt matched\'). Our reviews are 4.48 stars, below ggari-shop 4.57 and fair-furniture 4.58, and some are vague (\'Shop honored the agreed price\').","expected":"Small gain in review quality and stars (+0.03 to +0.08). Slightly higher share, since buyers choose by reviews.","risk":"Little effect if the buyer\'s review prompt ignores the shop\'s text. A longer message may also confuse an 8B model if not templated.","honest":"The message only restates true facts about the deal and asks for an honest review, with no incentive, no star conditioning and no scripted review.","test":"Compare mean stars and review count per deal for 24h before and after the change, with the template filled by code rather than the LLM.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"shop","title":"Fix the price gap on bookshelf and chair deals","what":"Check why our bookshelf deals close at 265-275 and chairs at 192 while the market median is 260 and 180 (middle half 260-264 and 180-182). Lower the target and opening ask for those two items to the market middle-half upper edge (264 and 182). Keep reserve + 5 as the floor. Leave lamps unchanged, since they already sit at the median of 100.","evidence":"Our reviews: \'bookshelf 265\', \'bookshelf 275\', \'chair at 192 | price 3/5 (median 186)\', and a 3* \'[no deal] chair: came down 192->185, then held; its floor was above my limit\'. Going prices show chair 180-182 and bookshelf 260-264. Our buyer\'s own data shows psyoung82 selling at 270 while others sold at 260.","expected":"Fewer no-deal visits (deals/visits is 4256/5500 = 77%, versus 99% for dragonash and ggari-shop) and fewer 3-star price reviews. Per-deal margin falls by about 5-10 credits, but volume and share should rise.","risk":"Margin drops on bookshelf and chair deals. If the reserve is above 260, the shop cannot match the median at all.","honest":"It is a pricing change within the floor and sells at real market prices. No claim about price is made.","test":"Run for 2-3 meta_shop cycles with the new targets for those two items. Compare conversion (deals/visits), average price and stars against the previous 24h.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Opening offer near the going price when budget allows","what":"Start offers at the going median for the item (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) when budget is at least that, instead of starting low and raising 40% of the gap. The cap of 1.1 x upper quartile and the budget stay unchanged. Where shops post an ask at or near the median, take it in one or two offers.","evidence":"Our buyer paid 84.6% of budget on average over 29 deals, but the 11 no-deals are all low-budget runs (lamp budget 90, chair budget 160) that walked. psyoung82\'s bookshelf took 7 offers 258->270 and finished at 270 when others paid 260, so slow raises can cost more than going straight to the median.","expected":"Fewer wasted offers and slightly fewer overpayments in chains like psyoung82\'s. Little change in no-deal rate for budgets below price.","risk":"Paying 100 for a lamp that could sell at 97. Median-opening may leave a few credits on the table with shops that concede.","honest":"The buyer offers a real price within its own budget and does not mislead the shop.","test":"A/B on half of trades: compare the average price paid versus the median and offers per deal across 40 trades.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Skip shops with a poor conversion record for low-budget tasks","what":"For tasks whose budget is below the going median, send them only to shops whose public ledger shows a low-price history, or skip them, rather than visiting shops that walked us. Save a per-shop \'walked at budget X\' record in buyer memory and avoid retrying a shop at a budget lower than its last walk.","evidence":"11 no-deals: cheapest-in-town chair budget 160 walked twice, dragonash lamp 90 and chair 160 walked, lee-sungsoo chair 160 walked, fair-furniture lamp 90 walked. Medians are 180/100 and the floors are 100 for yms979, so these budgets are below every price (rule: three offers then walk).","expected":"Fewer wasted visits and failed trades, with deal rate over 40 trades up from 29/40 where a budget can be met by anyone, and fewer walks logged against shops.","risk":"Low-budget tasks may be unmeetable anywhere, so the memory only saves effort rather than creating deals. Memory entries could go stale as prices move.","honest":"This is only the buyer\'s own bookkeeping. It makes no claims and attacks nobody.","test":"Count walks per 40 trades and the share of attempts with budget below the median before and after, using memory keyed by shop, item and budget.","effort":"small code"}]}'
