eroffer: 100.\' so the floor is about 100. These budgets were below every price.", "expected": "Fewer wasted visits and fewer walked trades. Since a budget of 90 cannot buy a 100 lamp, the saving is time and no failed-trade record, and the deal rate on feasible trades stays high (33 of 40 now).", "risk": "We might skip a shop that would have gone lower for a rare bargain, or the price in memory may be out of date.", "honest": "The buyer is not offering above its own budget and is not misleading shops. It simply stops negotiating where the evidence shows a deal is not possible.", "test": "Count visits per trade and the walked share over the next 100 trades. The deal rate on trades where budget >= the going price should stay at or above the current level.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Prefer shops that quote the going price on the first message", "what": "Add a feature to the buyer\'s pick score: the shop\'s first-quote gap to the going median, recorded in our own memory. Prefer shops whose first quote is at or below the median (for example dragonash opens 101 for a lamp, ggari-shop 101) and de-prioritise those that open above it or push an \'instant receipt\' (mjforge). Start with a small weight so reviews and ledger still dominate.", "evidence": "dragonash review: \'lamp at 100 (71% of budget), opened at 101, deal after 2 offers\'. mjforge review 3*: \'Opening ask was 186 (market average 185.7), and the shop\'s only message nudged me to accept it for an instant receipt rather than negotiate.\' Our buyer pays 85.9% of budget on average, so there is room to improve.", "expected": "Lower paid-to-budget ratio, for example from 85.9% toward 80%, and fewer offers per deal. The cost is a small drop in the set of shops we choose from.", "risk": "The first-quote data is thin for the smaller shops, so the score can be noisy, and a good shop that opens high might be missed.", "honest": "It uses facts the buyer observed itself. The buyer writes factual 