uyers hitting their three-offer walk limit, more deals per visit and about +0.05 stars.","risk":"Concedes slightly more on some deals; a faster first jump could hand buyers a lower price than they would have paid.","honest":"Just a different concession schedule on our own price; reserve + 5 is still enforced in code and nothing false is said.","test":"Split by item or by hour, compare mean offers per deal, speed sub-score, deal rate per visit, average realised price relative to median.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Open near the going median and stop wasting offers on shops that quote above it","what":"Make the buyer\'s first offer about the median (or 2-3% below, within the middle-half band) rather than a deep lowball, skip shops whose ask is above the item\'s band top, and cap the number of steps so we don\'t climb 260→270 in seven increments. Prefer shops whose median matches the market (ggari-shop, dragonash, fair-furniture, cheapest-in-town, lee-sungsoo) over psyoung82, yms979 and 11im.","evidence":"Buyer log: two psyoung82 bookshelf buys paid 270 \'it sold at 260 to others\' after asks [270 x7] and offers [260,264,...,270]. yms979 (chair 187, lamp 105, bookshelf 270) and 11im (lamp 105, bookshelf 270, chair 195) have medians above the market\'s 100/180/260. 13 no-deal trades included walks at fair-furniture, ggari-shop, dragonash and lee-sungsoo with low budgets (e.g. lamp budget 90, bookshelf budget 230), which cannot be won.","expected":"Pay about 260 instead of 270 on bookshelves (3-4% saving), a higher deal rate on the 27/40 baseline, and fewer wasted rounds; mean spend as share of budget drops below 85.4% where that is possible.","risk":"A first offer at the median may leave money on the table versus a shop that would accept less; skipping more shops may leave a lack of options at busy times.","honest":"Uses only public price data and our own memory, offers stay within the budget and nothing is misrepresented to the shop.","test":"Compa