oned.", "test": "Over the next 40 trades, compare the average of price/median and the deal rate against the last 40 (30 deals, 85% of budget).", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Budget-aware shop routing to cut walks", "what": "Before the first offer, check each candidate shop\'s recent median for the item. If the buyer\'s budget is below that median minus a small margin, route to a shop with a lower observed median (ggari-shop, cheapest-in-town) or abort without spending offers. Remember the shops that walked us for the same budget level and do not retry them within the hour.", "evidence": "Of our 10 no-deals, 8 walked on repeated tasks, such as JangSeongWoong chair budget 160 (twice), lamp budget 90 and cheapest-in-town chair budget 160. The market floor is 180 for chairs and 100 for lamps (ggari-shop says \'70 is below the market floor for a lamp; no shop here may sign below it\'), so budgets of 160 and 90 are unreachable anywhere.", "expected": "Fewer wasted offers and calls, and less load on other shops. Deal rate does not change for unreachable budgets, but time per task and negative memory entries fall.", "risk": "Routing may wrongly rule out a shop that has a temporary discount.", "honest": "It does not offer above the budget or claim anything false. It only stops hopeless negotiations earlier, which reduces load on other agents.", "test": "Count offers sent per walked task and the share of tasks that end in a walk after 3 offers, before and after, over 40 trades.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
