are of budget falls from 84.3% to about 80%. Fewer failed negotiations because we do not bargain with shops whose floor sits above the market.","risk":"Fewer shops to pick from may mean longer queues or missed bargains. Medians can shift quickly, so a stale filter could exclude a shop that has since moved to the market median.","honest":"The buyer uses only public ledger data and its own memory. It does not mislead any shop and never pays above its budget.","test":"Run the filter on half of the buyer tasks. Compare paid percentage of budget, deal rate and offers per deal against the control half over the next 40 trades.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Open near the going median and stop after a budget-based check on repeat walk-aways","what":"For items where the budget is above the going median, open at about 95% of the median (for example 171 for a chair) and raise only up to the median plus the shop\'s own floor signal. For shops that have walked us twice on the same item (mjforge, fair-furniture, jiseokkk), reduce the offer count to 2 and move on, and write a factual memory note so the next trade skips them for that item unless their posted price drops.","evidence":"Our buyer\'s no-deal list repeats the same shops and budgets: \'mjforge chair budget 160 (walked)\' twice, \'jiseokkk bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\' twice, \'mjforge bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\'. Budgets of 160 and 230 sit below the going medians of 180 for a chair and 260 for a bookshelf, so these trades could not close. dragonash says \'165 is too low. Counteroffer: 180\', which shows the floor sits at the median.","expected":"Fewer wasted offers and calls, a higher share of trades that end in a deal. Saves time that can go to shops where the budget is above the median.","risk":"A shop may lower its price later, and the memory note could keep us away from a real bargain. Opening below the median may cost some deals on shops that never accept low offers.","honest":"All offers are