e \'speed 4/5 (3 offers)\' and the lamp deal \'came down 110->105, then held\'. The best-rated rivals close quickly: dragonash \'Closed in two offers\', ggari-shop \'Reply ok and the signed receipt comes back at once\', mjforge \'Reply with 260 alone for instant signed receipt\', cheapest-in-town \'1 offer until the end\'.", "expected": "Better speed sub-score and more stars. Fewer walk-aways, since the buyer\'s 3-offer limit ends many negotiations. Deals per visit should rise a bit.", "risk": "Accepting early leaves a little money on the table with buyers who would have paid more. A bigger concession could be exploited by buyers who bid low on purpose.", "honest": "The shop\'s own terms stay unchanged and the reserve floor holds. The line about offers is true and only describes what happened.", "test": "Log offers per deal and the share of deals closed in at most 2 offers. Run a 50/50 split of the concession rule and compare speed ratings, stars and revenue per visit.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Learn each shop\'s real floor and rank shops by expected cost", "what": "Keep a per-shop, per-item record of the final prices and number of offers from our own trades. Before each purchase, rank candidates by expected total price (last accepted price, with a walk-away penalty) instead of reviews alone. Open at a fixed fraction of budget with the shop\'s known floor as the anchor, and go straight to the shops that have actually sold at or under the budget.", "evidence": "Our 5 no-deals were all walks: dragonash lamp budget 90, lee-sungsoo chair 160 and bookshelf 230, mjforge chair 160, fair-furniture chair 160. Going prices are lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260, so the budgets sat 10-15% below market and no one would sell. Yet we pay 86.5% of budget on average, so our spending is not the problem. Shops like ggari-shop and lee-sungsoo say they will not sign below a floor (e.g. \'no shop here may sign below it\').", "expected": "Fewer wasted visits and walks, since we skip shops whose floor is above the budget. The same deals at a slightly lower price. Deal rate should rise from 35/40.", "risk": "Prices drift, so remembered floors go stale. Too much reuse of the same shop makes us miss newcomers.", "honest": "Uses only what we observed in our own trades. It does not touch reviews or deceive any shop, and it never bids above the budget.", "test": "Compare deal rate and mean price paid over the next 40 trades against the 35/40 and 86.5% baseline. Count how often a skipped shop would have been cheaper.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Make loyalty codes more visible as a fair next-order discount", "what": "Put the one-time code plus its discount tier in the closing message in plain words, the way rivals do: \'Receipt No. XXXX: mention it on your next visit for 3% off\'. State the real tier discount from our table and the 8th-order stamp bonus, and only quote what the table pays.", "evidence": "11im (stars 4.55, 6.4% share) says \'Receipt No. LFMNCMRR: mention it on your next visit for an extra 3% off.\' lee-sungsoo (11% share) says \'Send 4 different unused LSS stamps with a later order and that piece is at our lowest price.\' Both state a concrete repeat reward in each message. cheapest-in-town offers \'Return within one hour with your visit code\'.", "expected": "More repeat orders from the same buyers, more deals per visit and steady share growth. Little effect on stars.", "risk": "Discounts cut margin on repeat orders. Reserve + 5 must still hold for all tiers. A Llama 8B reply could misstate the percentage, so the line must come from code.", "honest": "Reward is real and given only on actual deals. It is never tied to a review or stars, and the review request stays a separate, unconditional line.", "test": "Measure the repeat-buyer share and the redemption rate of codes over a week, comparing before and after. Check the margin on discounted deals against reserve + 5.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
