 "evidence": "Reviews of top shops praise the matched receipt: \'Clean deal: the chair was sold for 181 in one offer, and the signed receipt matched the final offer and ordered item\' (dragonash) and \'Signed receipt matched the deal\' (cheapest-in-town). Our reviews already show \'receipt matched\' wording, so buyers value it, and our stars (4.49) trail only ggari (4.55), 11im (4.54) and fair-furniture (4.57).", "expected": "Slightly higher stars through clearer receipt verification by buyer agents, and fewer 3-star reviews that mention a mismatch.", "risk": "Small effect, since our receipts are already signed and match. A rigid template could sound robotic, with no real downside to deals.", "honest": "The text states only facts about the real receipt. It asks for an honest review without conditioning anything on stars.", "test": "Compare the share of 5-star reviews and reviews mentioning \'receipt matched\' before and after, over about 500 deals.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Raise to the market band before walking", "what": "When the budget is at or above the band floor (260/180/100) and the shop\'s price is within the band, make the final offer at the middle-half band top (265/181/102) instead of walking after three offers. Prefer shops whose public medians are at the band.", "evidence": "Our buyer had 10 no-deals out of 40, including \'dragonash bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\' twice and \'cheapest-in-town bookshelf budget 230\'. Those budgets are below the 260 median, so these walks are expected. But \'fair-furniture chair budget 160 (walked)\' and \'lee-sungsoo lamp budget 90 (walked)\' are also below the going 180 and 100. Where budgets are at or above the band, the top shops accept at 180-181 and 100-102.", "expected": "Fewer walks where a deal is possible and a higher deal rate, at slightly higher paid prices, but never above budget or 1.1 x upper quartile.", "risk": "Might overpay by 1-2 credits where a shop would have accepted less. Walking when the budget is below the band stays correct and is unchanged.", "honest": "It pays a real market price within budget. No claims are made to the shop.", "test": "Log walk reasons and compare deal rate and paid/budget ratio over the next 40 trades against the previous 40.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Skip shops on budget-below-band without trying", "what": "Before a visit, compare budget to the band floor for the item. If the budget is below it, go first to shops with a lower public median, such as none in this evidence, or skip quickly and record \'budget below market\' rather than spending three offers. Use the saved visit effort for tasks that can close.", "evidence": "Walked tasks with budgets 160 (chair), 90 (lamp) and 230 (bookshelf, three times) all sit below the going prices of 180, 100 and 260, and the top shops all show the same medians. One run also ended in \'outseop-26510122 bookshelf budget 230 (error)\'.", "expected": "Saves calls and time, reduces error exposure and rate-limit pressure, and keeps the paid/budget ratio unaffected. The deal count will not rise for these tasks, but throughput on feasible tasks will.", "risk": "A shop might occasionally sell below the median, so skipping could miss a rare deal. Keeping one cheap probe offer at the budget limits the loss.", "honest": "It reduces load on other agents and makes no claims. Offers stay within the budget.", "test": "Count tasks skipped versus later-deal outcomes using a 10% random probe sample, and compare calls per deal.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
