[11:23:15] HARNESS: try 1: 16.9 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas":[{"side":"shop","title":"Close faster: fewer offers to deal on near-target bids","what":"Raise the concession from 40% to about 60% of the gap, only when the buyer\'s offer is within ~3% of target, and never below reserve+5. Aim to close in 1-2 offers like dragonash (lamp deal in 2 offers) and ggari-shop, which counters near the buyer\'s offer.","evidence":"Our share is 2.8% (7482 deals / 9531 visits = 78% conversion) versus ggari-shop 12.4% (79% conversion) and dragonash 11.2% (76%), so the gap is in visits, not conversion. Our 3* review says \'came down 275->265, then held ... its floor was above my limit\', and another 3* was priced at 185 against a going chair price of 180. Our chair deals land at 182-192, above the 180 median, while the top shops close at 180.","expected":"Fewer no-deal and 3* reviews, a slightly higher star average and more visits from review-driven buyers. Each deal may give up 1-3 credits, offset by a higher close rate on bookshelves.","risk":"Margin erosion if the buyer would have paid more. Faster concessions could also teach buyers to wait.","honest":"Only the price schedule changes. The floor stays at reserve+5 and nothing said to the buyer is false.","test":"A/B by task id parity for 24h: compare conversion, average margin, share of 3* reviews and visits per hour for bookshelf and chair.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"shop","title":"Match the market median on chairs and bookshelves in the opening ask and target","what":"Cap the opening ask at the market\'s middle-half upper bound (chair 182, bookshelf 266, lamp 103) and set the target to the median (180/260/100) whenever the reserve allows. This is a change to meta_shop.py, using the 3-hour quartiles it already reads.","evidence":"Going prices: chair 180 (middle half 180-182), bookshelf 260 (260-266), lamp 100 (100-103). Our chair deals were 185, 185, 192 and 182. Our buyers skip shops above the upper quartile, and the 3* review said we held 265 on a bookshelf where the budget was lower. Top shops all show medians of exactly 180/260/100.","expected":"More visits for us from buyers who filter by price and get picked by review-plus-price. Per-deal revenue drops a little. The share gain should come from more visits.","risk":"If our reserve is above the median on some items we would lose those deals or sell at the reserve+5 margin. The price war could drag all medians down.","honest":"Prices are real asks and targets, never below reserve+5, and there is no invented \'was\' price.","test":"Compare visits per 15-minute window and share before and after the change over 6h, and log the margin per item.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"shop","title":"Say plainly on the first reply what the receipt records and what the floor is","what":"Add the line \'Signed receipt records item, price and task; no added fees\' to the opening ask and deal messages, with the deal price and offer count (e.g. \'Deal: chair at 180, closed in 2 offers\'). Keep the existing thank-you and honest-review request.","evidence":"Top shops state it: dragonash \'Closed in two offers. Your receipt is signed by the shop, so this deal counts\'; cheapest-in-town \'Zero added fees; your signed receipt records this exact item, price and task\'; psyoung82 repeats the same. Reviews of those shops then say \'signed receipt matched the deal\' and \'receipt matched the final offer and ordered item\', which are the facts the buyers score on.","expected":"More 4-5* reviews that mention matching receipts, improving what review-based buyers read, and a higher star average (ours is 4.49 versus 4.56 for fair-furniture).","risk":"Longer messages cost a few tokens on the 8B model. If the code adds the text, the model could contradict it.","honest":"Each statement is true because the code actually produces signed receipts with these fields, and no review is requested in return for anything.","test":"Compare the share of reviews that mention \'receipt matched\' and mean stars over the next 1000 deals against the previous 1000.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Retry a different shop instead of walking after a failed negotiation","what":"When the buyer walks or is rejected (all 6 of the last no-deals), do one fallback visit to the next-best shop that is not above the upper quartile, with the same budget and the same 40% raise limit. Remember which shops refused at which budget in own memory.","evidence":"Last 40 trades: 6 no-deals, and 5 of 6 were at lee-sungsoo, ggari-shop or dragonash, with budgets 160 and 230 for chair and bookshelf (the going price for a chair is 180, for a bookshelf 260). A budget of 160 for a chair is below every shop\'s price, but the 230 bookshelf budgets are likewise below the 260 median. The buyer also paid 85.7% of budget on average, so it is not overpaying.","expected":"Recover some of the 15% of trades that end without a deal, with no change in price paid. Better data for memory about which shops sell below the median.","risk":"If the budget is below every price, a retry wastes visits and can look like overloading. Visits could also lower the buyer\'s score.","honest":"The buyer never offers above its budget, never exceeds 1.1x the upper quartile, and a second visit is one ordinary negotiation. Reviews stay factual.","test":"Count the deal rate after fallback versus the 85% baseline, keeping retries to at most one per task, and compare average paid versus budget.","effort":"small code"}]}'
