our receipt is signed by the shop, so this deal counts. Please leave an honest review.\') and 25620027\'s (\'Completed in 4 buyer offers. Seller-signed receipt matches...\') are matched by reviews that quote the same facts, e.g. \'Paid 260 of 295\' and \'receipt matched the agreed price\'. Our own reviews are already detailed, but several are 4* and mention only the receipt. dragonash has 32,471 reviews for 32,719 deals.", "expected": "Review rate nearer 99% (ours is 7867/7868, already high) and more informative reviews. Small gain in stars and in the ledger signal buyers use. Little effect on share by itself.", "risk": "Longer messages can confuse the 8B model, which may garble the price. Little upside because our review rate is already at its ceiling.", "honest": "States only true facts about the deal and asks for an honest review with no incentive or conditions.", "test": "Template the closing in code rather than the LLM. Compare stars, review length and review rate before and after, over 1000 deals.", "effort": "small code"},\n{"side": "buyer", "title": "Skip the walk-away when a shop\'s floor is within budget plus 10%; try a second shop first", "what": "When the budget is below every shop\'s price, do not run three offers at the first shop. Do one probe offer at budget at each of the top 3 shops by reviews, and use a shop\'s own stated floor (for example \'our floor: 100\') as the signal to stop. Otherwise stay within the 1.1 x upper-quartile cap.", "evidence": "Our buyer\'s 5 failures: lee-sungsoo lamp budget 90 (walked), dragonash lamp budget 90 (walked), fair-furniture chair budget 160 (walked). The lamp market median is 100 (middle half 100-105) and the chair median is 180. These budgets are 10-11% below the going price, so no shop would sell. yms979 says \'This is our floor, we never sell below it: 100\'. Also 35 of 40 deals closed at 81.6% of budget.", "expected": "The number of deals is unchanged, since those tasks cannot be filled below market. Offers and time per walked task drop; deals paid at over 80% of budget may go down slightly if we skip shops that would sell lower.", "risk": "If a shop would have sold below the median after more offers, we lose that discount. Hitting the cap on the cheapest shop first may reduce choice.", "honest": "We only make real offers within the budget and stop when a shop says its floor is above it. No pressure and no extra load on the shops.", "test": "Log offers per task and outcome for walked tasks. Compare offers used and deal rate over 100 tasks with the old and new rule.", "effort": "small code"},\n{"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the going-price median minus a small step, not at a budget fraction", "what": "Open the first offer at the 3-hour median price (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) when the budget is at or above it. Offer exactly the median first and keep the raise rule of 40% of the gap. Where a shop\'s reply states a price at or below the median, accept it immediately.", "evidence": "Going prices: lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260, and the middle half is a narrow band of 5. Leaders close at exactly these numbers: ggari-shop \'Deal at 100 for a lamp (asked 105) after 2 offers\', mjforge \'Deal at 260 for bookshelf (budget 325)\', cheapest-in-town \'Lamp at 100, budget 110. 1 offer\'. Our buyer pays 81.6% of budget, while fair-furniture\'s reviews show 88% of budget and 1 offer.", "expected": "Fewer rounds and fewer walk-aways, and an average price near the median instead of drifting up to 1.1 x the quartile. Savings of about 0-5 credits per deal.", "risk": "Some shops, such as 11im with a median of 104-270, price above the median, so an opening at the median fails there and costs one extra offer.", "honest": "We offer a price taken from public market data and never exceed the budget. Our reviews state the price paid truthfully.", "test": "Alternate the opening rule by task and compare the average paid as a share of the market median and offers per deal over 200