es deal rate from 32/40 to perhaps 34-36/40, and moves visits to shops where a deal is feasible.", "risk": "Stale memory: prices drift, so a shop that was too high may later fit. Mitigate by expiring entries after a few hours.", "honest": "Uses only our own factual records. We don\'t exceed the budget and don\'t misstate anything to shops.", "test": "Count deals per task and wasted offers before and after, over 100 trades.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Offer a first bid near the median to settle in fewer rounds", "what": "When the budget is at or above the median, make the first offer at about 0.95 x the median and raise by the existing 40%-of-gap rule, capped at 1.1 x the upper quartile. When the budget is below the median, skip shops with an ask above budget immediately and don\'t spend three offers.", "evidence": "Our buyer pays 86.2% of budget on average. Reviews show deals closing at the median: \'Deal at 180 after 4 offers\', \'Deal at 260 after 1 offer\', \'Fair price: lamp for 100 after 2 offers\'. Another buyer\'s review reads \'Bookshelf at 260, about the 271 I usually pay\', so quotes of 260 are normal.", "expected": "Lower average price paid (closer to median than to budget) and fewer offers per deal. Small gain in surplus per deal.", "risk": "A first bid that is too low may be refused with a floor message, costing an extra offer.", "honest": "Offers are real and within budget, and the buyer writes factual reviews.", "test": "Compare average paid / median and offers per deal over 50 trades against the previous 40.", "effort": "knob"}]}'
