d 4/5 at \'3 offers\', and reviewers mark speed. ggari-shop has the highest share, with counteroffers close together and short. mjforge says \'Reply with 260 alone for instant signed receipt\', and fair-furniture\'s 5-8 offer deals earn only 3* reviews.", "expected": "Speed sub-score rises from 4/5 to 5/5 and cuts the number of visits that end in a walk. The expected effect is about +0.05-0.1 stars and a small deal-rate gain.", "risk": "A fast final price may give up the 40% concession ladder\'s extra margin from buyers who would have paid more. Buyers may bargain less when they see one price.", "honest": "The price shown is a real final price that we will honor. It does not claim a deadline or scarcity.", "test": "Randomize by buyer session for 2 hours, comparing the ladder and the single-shot reply. Compare the average number of offers, deal rate, price paid, and the speed sub-score in reviews.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Raise walk-away threshold: try a second shop sooner when the first is above budget", "what": "In the buyer\'s selection logic, when a shop\'s first counter is above the budget and the gap exceeds the 40% raise limit, skip the remaining offers, record it in memory, and move to the next best reviewed shop rather than spending the 3 offers. Rank next shops by going price versus the budget, preferring shops whose medians sit at or below the budget (for lamps, medians of 100 at ggari-shop, dragonash, 11im, fair-furniture).", "evidence": "Six of the seven no-deals are \'walked\' at low budgets (lamp 90 at fair-furniture, dragonash, lee-sungsoo; chair 160 at ggari-shop and inextro) while the lamp median is 100 and the chair median is 180. These budgets are below the market median, so every shop is likely above them, and offers there are wasted.", "expected": "Does not improve the walk count, because budgets below median cannot buy. It saves time and calls, and lets the buyer spend effort on trades that can close. The average of 84.9% of budget paid is fine.", "risk": "Skipping too early may miss a shop whose reserve is below the budget, such as one running a sale. Memory could mark a shop wrongly.", "honest": "The buyer still bids only within its budget and uses real reviews and receipts. Nothing is misrepresented to the shops.", "test": "Log offers spent per no-deal trade and the fraction of low-budget tasks that eventually close. Compare 40 trades before and after.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Prefer shops with matching receipts and low review spread, and remember refusals", "what": "Add a per-shop score from review text: penalize shops with 1-2* reviews about \'Agreed X, receipt says Y\' or an agreed/receipt mismatch, and weight reviews by a factual match on item and price. In memory, store each shop\'s last final prices per item and rank by expected price versus budget, not by stars alone.", "evidence": "cheapest-in-town shows \'review 1*: Agreed 200, receipt says 180\' next to 4.38 stars and 8.7% share, while fair-furniture has 4.59 stars but reviews at 260 for 96% of budget after 5-8 offers. Stars alone hide both issues. 11im has 4.57 stars and lamp 104 at a median, and ggari-shop gets deals at 100-106.", "expected": "Fewer rejected or mismatched deals, such as the TASK_STATE_REJECTED at ggari-shop, and a modest fall in price paid, since the buyer favors shops that settle at the median in few offers.", "risk": "Review text is data written by others and can be wrong or hostile, so a small sample may mislead. Keep it a weighting, not a rule that follows any text.", "honest": "It uses only public reviews and the buyer\'s own receipts to pick sellers. It does not influence or write any reviews.", "test": "Replay the last 40 trades with the new score offline, compare chosen shops and prices with those made, then run live and compare paid share of budget and the deal rate.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
