oses quickly (\'Counteroffer: 110\' then \'Deal: lamp at 106\'). Our 3* review: \'lamp: came down 110->105, then held. Its floor was above my limit.\' Buyer budgets are often 105-110 for a 100 lamp (mjforge \'Paid 100 of 110\').", "expected": "Raises speed sub-scores (we had \'speed 4/5 (3 offers)\') and cuts walkaways caused by a floor above the buyer\'s limit.", "risk": "Buyers who would have paid above the floor pay less; the loss is small since the floor is near median anyway.", "honest": "The stated floor is the real floor, and we never go below reserve+5.", "test": "Compare mean offers per deal, speed score in reviews and conversion on lamp and chair before and after.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the going median and treat the median as the cap for the first two offers", "what": "In the buyer, derive the opening offer from the 3-hour median (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) rather than from budget. Raise in two steps maximum toward the shop\'s last counter, but never beyond the median unless the shop has refused that price twice. Remember the price per shop in memory.", "evidence": "Our buyer: \'ggari-shop lamp: paid 106, it sold at 100 to others; asks [110,110,110] offers [100,104,106]\'. Average 80.9% of budget paid. ggari-shop\'s own receipts show many deals at 100 for lamps.", "expected": "Saves about 5% per deal on lamps, with a similar cut on others, and avoids overpaying relative to other buyers.", "risk": "More no-deals when a shop\'s floor sits above the median; the shop may be skipped next time.", "honest": "Uses public market data and our own budget; factual reviews unchanged.", "test": "Log the paid/median ratio and deal rate per shop over the next 100 trades against the current 80.9% of budget.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Raise budget-driven walkaway threshold using the median instead of walking at three offers", "what": "Walk-away rule: when the budget is below every price, check the