e lamps at 100-101 and 5* reviews cite \'asked 105... deal at 100\'. Median lamp is 100 with a middle half of 100-105.", "expected": "Higher price score, more 4-5* reviews, and a higher conversion rate. Share should move from 2.6% toward the 6-10% held by shops that close at the median. Per-deal margin drops by 5 or less on lamps.", "risk": "If reserve is near 100, margin is nearly gone. The buyer may still walk at budgets below 100 (budget 75-90 cases cannot be served anyway).", "honest": "It is just a real lower price. The ask is not inflated and nothing is claimed about a previous price.", "test": "A/B by item over 24h: lamp target 100 vs 105. Compare deal rate, stars, and margin per visit.", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Make the receipt the first line and state fees honestly in every deal message", "what": "Change the deal template to: \'Deal: <item> at <price>. Zero added fees; signed receipt attached; it records the exact item, price and task.\' Then add the honest-review request. Keep the thank-you short so a small model does not drift. Generate the deal line from code, not from the LLM.", "evidence": "The top shops\' deal lines (cheapest-in-town \'Zero added fees; your signed receipt records this exact item, price and task\'; dragonash \'Your receipt is signed by the shop, so this deal counts\'; psyoung82 \'no added charges\') are code-like and consistent. Reviews score \'receipt 5/5 (matched, signed)\', and Round 2 counts only signed receipts.", "expected": "Small gain in the buyers\' receipt and conduct scores and fewer ambiguous reviews. Slightly higher stars and a better chance of being picked by review-reading buyers.", "risk": "Little effect if the receipt is already clear. Longer text costs a few tokens.", "honest": "Every statement is true: the receipt is signed and there are no extra fees. It asks for an honest review without conditioning anything on it.", "test": "Compare the share of reviews that mention the receipt and the average receipt/conduct subscore over 24h before and after.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open near the median and accept a counter at or below budget after fewer offers", "what": "Make the buyer\'s first offer about 90-92% of the going median, not a fraction of budget, and when a shop\'s counter is at or below the middle-half upper edge and within budget, accept it instead of raising by 40% of the gap. Prefer shops whose ledger shows closes at the median (ggari-shop, dragonash). Keep a walk rule only when budget is below every price.", "evidence": "Our buyer walked 6 of the last 8 non-deals (fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230, lee-sungsoo chair budget 160, and others), and paid 85.3% of budget on average. Those budgets are below the median of 260 and 180, so walking is right. But fair-furniture needed 6-7 offers to settle at 260, and ggari-shop and dragonash close at the median in about 2 offers.", "expected": "Fewer offers per deal, fewer timeouts and errors, and about the same price (median or a little under). Deals should rise a few points above 32 of 40.", "risk": "A lower opening offer could cost a deal against firm shops. Accepting early could leave a few credits on the table.", "honest": "The buyer only offers its own real budget-limited prices and writes factual reviews. No claims are made to the shop.", "test": "Run for 40 trades and compare deals, average paid/budget, and offers per deal against the 32/40 and 85.3% baseline.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
