ere the middle half is 260-270. Reviews of ggari-shop say \'Came down to 100 for the lamp after 2 offer(s)\'.", "expected": "More 5* reviews referencing fair negotiation; average price roughly unchanged (about 1-2 credits lower on some deals), stars up slightly.", "risk": "Gives away a few credits on deals that would have closed at target anyway; margin loss of 1-2%.", "honest": "Real concessions at real prices above reserve; no fake \'was\' price or scarcity.", "test": "Randomise 50% of items to the variant; compare average sale price, star distribution and deal count per 1000 visits.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Fall back to the next cheapest shop instead of walking when the first fails", "what": "When our buyer\'s three offers fail at shop A and the budget is below every price at that shop, move to the next-ranked shop in the same task (if the task allows) rather than ending without a deal. Prefer shops with a visible floor message (yms979, ggari-shop) whose floor is at or near the median.", "evidence": "13 of our last 40 trades ended without a deal, six listed as walked or rejected (jiseokkk chair budget 160, fair-furniture bookshelf budget 230, dragonash lamp budget 90, inextro chair 160, lee-sungsoo bookshelf 230). Going prices (chair median 180, bookshelf 260) exceed those budgets, so walking is expected there; but the failures listed are at shops whose medians are the market medians.", "expected": "Fewer no-deal trades where a deal below budget is attainable; a possible 5-10 point rise in the deal rate on cases where budget >= market median.", "risk": "If the budget is truly below every market price, extra visits waste time. Spending more visits can also add load on shops.", "honest": "Normal shopping across shops; no overloading, and offers stay within budget and the 1.1x upper-quartile cap.", "test": "Log walked trades, replay with the fallback on half of them, and compare the deal rate and percent of budget paid.", "effort": "sma