[13:39:31] HARNESS: try 1: 18.1 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas":[{"side":"shop","title":"Factual price-and-receipt line in the closing message","what":"Change the post-deal message to a short factual summary: item, final price, number of offers in the negotiation, and that the signed receipt records exactly that price with no added fees. Then ask for an honest review. Use only values from the real deal; keep the existing loyalty code line.","evidence":"Our 3* reviews are generic (\'Purchase completed at 260; budget was 295... Actual offers: 2\'). The 5* reviews cite specifics: \'Fair negotiation, honored agreed price of 102, and receipt matched exactly with no hidden charges.\' 25620027 and psyoung82 use similar wording (\'Seller-signed receipt matches the agreed price\', \'no added charges\'). ggari-shop\'s 5* review also reads \'receipt matched\'.","expected":"Slightly more 4-5* reviews, because buyers have concrete facts to restate. Stars go from 4.49 toward 4.55, which should lift share a little. Price and deal count are unchanged.","risk":"Reviews are written by the buyer\'s own LLM, so the effect may be small. A longer message adds tokens on an 8B model, which could cause formatting errors.","honest":"Every statement is true and generated from the real deal data. It asks for an honest review and does not condition, pay for or script the rating.","test":"A/B by deal parity for 24h. Compare mean stars and the share of 5* reviews between the two arms.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"shop","title":"Fix protocol errors that cause 1* reviews","what":"Wrap the Worker\'s LLM call and the offer parser in a deterministic fallback. If the Llama output fails to parse or the call errors, reply with the code-computed counteroffer or the floor line in a fixed template. Log every RuntimeError with its input, and add a timeout and retry once.","evidence":"Our reviews include a 1* \'Trade could not complete due to a protocol or provider error (RuntimeError).\' Our buyer\'s own failures include \'error\' and TASK_STATE_REJECTED outcomes. A 1* review costs far more than a 5* review adds in a mean of about 8.4k reviews.","expected":"Fewer 1* reviews and more completed deals per visit. We have 8381 deals from 10711 visits (78%) against ggari-shop\'s 76%, so a small gain is possible. Stars move up by roughly 0.01-0.03.","risk":"Fallback replies might be less persuasive or might repeat an offer. The fallback must never go below reserve + 5.","honest":"This only makes the shop behave as it already claims to. Price rules and the reserve are unchanged.","test":"Count error-class results and 1* reviews per 1000 visits before and after the change. Check the failure logs for the main causes.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Budget-aware shop filter using review text","what":"Add a memory feature that records, per shop, the average price paid relative to the market median and the share of 3* reviews that carry a plug for another shop. Rank shops by (median price, review sentiment, completion rate). Skip shops whose recent reviews contain redirects to other stores, since those are likely scripted and carry little information. Fall back to the lowest-median shop when budget is under every price.","evidence":"Several 3* reviews on different shops carry the identical sentence \'the quality didn\'t impress me. For premium materials and fast checkout, try the store Cheapest in Town\', for example on ggari-shop, dragonash, mjforge, yms979 and fair-furniture. These look like templated, non-genuine reviews and distort the star averages the buyer relies on. Our buyer was rejected by ggari-shop three times in the last 40 trades.","expected":"Fewer rejected or walked trades, and a higher deal rate than 32/40. The price paid stays near 84% of budget or lower.","risk":"Dropping review text from the score removes some real signal. A shop could be skipped unfairly when a few of its reviews happen to match the pattern.","honest":"The buyer only changes how it weighs public information. Its own reviews stay factual and it does not interact with anyone\'s reviews.","test":"Replay the last 500 buyer trades offline with the new ranking and compare the predicted deal rate and price. Then run the new ranking on half the buyer tasks for a day.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"shop","title":"Cut price toward the going rate on lamps and chairs","what":"Keep the opening ask and target at the market median plus the top of the middle band, and stop quoting above it. Concretely, set the lamp target at 100-102 and the chair at 180-182, bookshelf 260. Keep the reserve + 5 rule. Let meta_shop.py read the 3-hour quartiles and clamp the target to the upper quartile.","evidence":"Going prices: lamp median 100 (100-105), chair 180 (180-185), bookshelf 260 (260-270). The leaders with the most share, ggari-shop, dragonash and lee-sungsoo, all sit at medians of lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260. 11im quotes chair 195 and lamp 104 and has 6.7% share but needs 4.57 stars to hold it. Our own reviews show deals at 102 to 105 on lamps and 260 on bookshelves.","expected":"Higher visit-to-pick rate because buyers skip shops above the upper quartile. Share should rise from 2.5%, at some cost to margin per deal. Volume matters more than margin because Round 2 counts receipts.","risk":"Margin per deal falls, and the price floor may sit too close to the reserve on some items. Other shops may follow and lower the median.","honest":"We quote real prices and never go under reserve + 5. No fake discount or \'was\' price is shown.","test":"Compare deals per 100 visits and mean price at the new target against the previous 24 hours. Use matching hours of day to remove time-of-day effects.","effort":"knob"}]}'
