265, then held\'.", "expected": "Fewer buyer rounds, better \'speed\' scores in structured reviews (ours are 4/5 on 3 offers while yms979 gets 5/5 on 2 offers), slightly higher stars.", "risk": "A visible low ask can anchor buyers below our target, so test per item.", "honest": "States only the real current ask and our real process. Nothing about scarcity or deadlines.", "test": "Compare average offers per deal and the speed sub-score in reviews before and after for 6h windows. Check stars and the deal-to-visit ratio.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Cap concession rounds: fewer offers to the same final price", "what": "Make the code jump to the final price after the buyer\'s second offer when it is within 3-5% of our target. For example, \'This is my best price: X\' (as yms979 does) rather than conceding 40% of the gap each step. Never go below reserve + 5.", "evidence": "yms979 reviews: \'speed 5/5 (2 offers)\'. Ours: \'speed 4/5 (3 offers)\'. ggari-shop closes on a couple of counters (104, then 101-106) and has 38,374 reviews at 4.55 stars.", "expected": "Higher speed scores and stars, and a slightly higher deal-per-visit rate, because fewer buyers hit their offer limit. Cost is a few credits per deal on some buyers.", "risk": "Buyers who would have paid more are closed early at a lower price.", "honest": "\'Best price\' is only said when it is true: the code must set it to the actual final number and then hold it.", "test": "Randomize the concession rule by visit ID. Compare mean offers per deal, price/speed sub-scores in reviews, and revenue per visit.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Retry a walked or rejected deal with a different shop at once", "what": "When a shop rejects or the buyer walks (e.g. a bookshelf at 325 rejected by mjforge and dragonash), automatically retry with the next-best shop in the ledger rather than ending the task. Prefer shops whose medians are at or below the going price (ggari-shop, fair-furniture, cheapest-in-town). Keep the budget cap of 1.1 x upper quartile.", "evidence": "Our buyer: 8 of the last 40 trades ended without a deal. Examples: \'mjforge bookshelf budget 325 (TASK_STATE_REJECTED)\', \'dragonash bookshelf budget 325 (TASK_STATE_REJECTED)\', \'real_yellow bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\'. Going bookshelf price is 260, well within the 325 budgets, so rejection came from the shop, not the budget.", "expected": "Cut the 20% no-deal rate (8/40) toward 5-10%, and keep the 81.9% average budget use or lower.", "risk": "Extra visits add load and time, so cap retries at one or two. Budget 230 for a bookshelf cannot be met at 260, so skip retry in those cases.", "honest": "Only normal purchasing from other shops with the same budget and review rules. No extra load beyond one more visit, and no deal with ourselves.", "test": "Log the share of tasks that reach a deal with and without the retry, plus paid/budget, over the next 40 trades.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
